{{Campaignbox Vietnam War}}

At the start of 1971 [[South Vietnam]]ese troops continued operations against the [[North Vietnam]]ese [[People's Army of Vietnam]] (PAVN) and [[Vietcong]] (VC) base areas in eastern [[Khmer Republic|Cambodia]]. The ill-conceived and poorly executed [[Operation Lam Son 719]] against PAVN supply lines in eastern [[Kingdom of Laos|Laos]] showed the weaknesses within the South Vietnamese military command and the limited ability of South Vietnam's armed forces to conduct large-scale combined arms operations. The U.S. continued its unilateral withdrawal from South Vietnam despite the lack of any progress in the [[Paris Peace Talks]] and by November U.S. forces had ceased offensive operations. The U.S. withdrawal and antiwar sentiment within the military led to an ongoing decline in morale and discipline within the U.S. forces and growing drug use, particularly of heroin. As U.S. combat units withdrew, security in their former operational areas deteriorated and the PAVN/VC began a series of attacks on ARVN positions in [[Quảng Trị province]] and the [[Central Highlands (Vietnam)|Central Highlands]]. In Cambodia the Cambodian government continued to lose ground to the PAVN despite extensive U.S. air support and training and periodic attacks into Cambodia by the ARVN. While the bombing of North Vietnam had ceased in November 1968, U.S. aircraft continued to conduct reconnaissance flights over the North and responded to radar-tracking and antiaircraft fire with "protective reaction" strikes which numbered more than 100 by the year-end and culminated in a five-day bombing campaign in late December.

==January==
; 1 January
U.S. military personnel in South Vietnam totaled 334,600 on 31 December 1970.<ref name=Willbanks>{{cite book|last=Willbanks|first=James|title=Vietnam War Almanac: An In-Depth Guide to the Most Controversial Conflict in American History|publisher=Simon and Schuster|year=2013|isbn=9781626365285}}</ref>{{rp|359}}

;1 January - May 1971
[[Project Copper]] was an unsuccessful operation to use three [[Central Intelligence Agency]] (CIA)-trained [[Cambodia]]n irregular force battalions to interdict the [[Sihanouk Trail]]. One battalion deserted, one mutinied during training and the third suffered extensive casualties and had to be withdrawn to assist in the defense of [[Phnom Penh]].<ref name=Conboy>{{cite book|last1=Conboy|first1=Kenneth|last2=Morrison|first2=James|title=Shadow War: The CIA's Secret War in Laos|publisher=Paladin Press|year=1996|isbn=0-87364-825-0}}</ref>{{rp|282–4}}

;5 January
The [[United States Congress]] adopted the revised [[Cooper-Church Amendment]] which prohibited the introduction of U.S. ground troops or advisers into Cambodia and declared that U.S. aid to Cambodia should not be considered a commitment to the defense of Cambodia.<ref>[https://library.cqpress.com/cqalmanac/document.php?id=cqal70-1292681 CQ Almanac, accessed 10 Aug 2015]</ref>

;5 January - 11 February
[[Operation Silver Buckle]] was a [[Royal Lao Army]] (RLA) offensive staged in [[Military Regions of Laos|Military Region 4]] of [[Laos]] and was the deepest RLA penetration to date of the [[Ho Chi Minh Trail]]. Reaching the Trailside village of Moung Nong, the forward two companies attacked the rear of the 50,000 man [[People's Army of Vietnam]] (PAVN) garrison on 8 February 1971, just as [[Operation Lam Son 719]] was launched by the [[Army of the Republic of Vietnam]] (ARVN) and diverted at least six PAVN battalions away from the ARVN assault.<ref name=Conboy/>{{rp|286–90}}

; 5 January - 30 May
The ARVN launched Operation Toàn Thắng TT02 which culminated in the [[Battle of Snuol]] against PAVN and [[Viet Cong]] (VC) forces in the [[Snuol District]] of Cambodia. The PAVN/VC lost 1,043 killed while the ARVN lost 37 killed, 74 missing and more than 300 captured. The operation rendered the ARVN [[5th Division (South Vietnam)|5th Division]] combat ineffective.<ref name=Cosmas>{{cite book|last=Cosmas|first=Graham|title=The United States Army in Vietnam MACV The Joint Command in the Years of Withdrawal, 1968–1973|publisher=United States Army Center of Military History|year=2006|url=https://history.army.mil/html/books/macv2/index.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100608151805/http://www.history.army.mil/html/books/macv2/index.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=June 8, 2010|isbn=9780160771187}}{{PD-notice}}</ref>{{rp|338–9}}

; 6 January
U.S. [[United States Secretary of Defense|Secretary of Defense]] [[Melvin Laird]] said that the "[[Vietnamization]]" of the war was running ahead of schedule and that the combat mission of the U.S. troops would end in summer 1971.<ref>{{cite book|last=Daugherty|first=Leo|title=The Vietnam War Day by Day|publisher=Chartwell Books|year=2002|isbn=978-0785828570|page=177}}</ref>

[[Military Assistance Command Vietnam]] (MACV) announced a program to combat drug abuse among U.S. servicemen in South Vietnam with some 65,000 servicemen estimated to be drug abusers in 1970.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|359–60}}

; 7 January
[[File:'Ranch Hand' run.jpg|thumb|right|Operation Ranch Hand mission]]
The last herbicide spraying by the United States to defoliate forests in South Vietnam and kill crops used to feed communist soldiers and supporters was carried out in [[Ninh Thuan]] province. [[Operation Ranch Hand]] was finished.<ref>Buckingham, Jr., William A. (1982) ''Operation Ranch Hand: The U.S. Air Force and Herbicides, 1961-1971'' Washington, D.C.: Office of Air Force History, p. 175</ref>

;8 January
U.S. jets carried out "protective reaction" strikes attacking two North Vietnamese [[Surface to air missile]] sites north of the [[Vietnamese Demilitarized Zone]] (DMZ).<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|360}}

;11 January - 29 March
The [[1st Battalion 1st Marines]] conducted Operation Upshur Stream, continuous reconnaissance and infantry patrolling and concentrated air and artillery attacks in an effort to prevent the PAVN/VC from using the Charlie Ridge base area for attacks against [[Da Nang]].<ref name=USMC>{{cite book|last=Cosmas|first=Graham|title= U.S. Marines In Vietnam: Vietnamization And Redeployment, 1970-1971|publisher=USMC Military History Division|url=https://www.marines.mil/Portals/59/Publications/U.S.%20Marines%20In%20Vietnam_Vietmanization%20and%20Redeployment%201970-1971%20%20PCN%2019000309600.pdf?ver=2017-04-14-150918-977}}{{PD-notice}}</ref>{{rp|215}}

;12 January
A group of religious anti-war activists, led by [[Philip Berrigan]], who became known as the [[Harrisburg Seven]], were indicted on charges of planning to kidnap [[National Security Advisor]] [[Henry Kissinger]] and blow up steam tunnels under federal buildings in [[Washington D.C.]]<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|360}}

The [[Concerned Officers Movement]] held a press conference where they called for an investigation into the "responsibility for war crimes of key military figures", including Generals [[William Westmoreland]] and [[Creighton Abrams]] and Admiral [[Elmo Zumwalt]].<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|360}}

;13 January
[[President of the United States|President]] [[Richard Nixon]] signed legislation repealing the [[Gulf of Tonkin Resolution]] which had been used as the legal basis for U.S., involvement in the war.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Gulf of Tonkin Resolution Is Repealed Without Furor|date=14 January 1971|page=1}}</ref>

; 13 to 25 January
The ARVN 4th Armor Brigade and [[Vietnamese Rangers|4th Ranger Group]] and the [[Republic of Vietnam Marine Division|2nd Marine Brigade]] together with [[Khmer National Armed Forces]] (FANK) forces launched [[Operation Cuu Long 44-02]] to reopen [[National Highway 4 (Cambodia)|Route 4]] in Cambodia. The operation resulted in 211 PAVN and 16 ARVN killed.<ref name=Starry>{{cite book|last=Starry|first=Donn|title=Mounted Combat in Vietnam. Vietnam Studies|publisher=Department of the Army|year=1978|isbn=9781517592288|url=https://history.army.mil/html/books/090/90-17-1/CMH_Pub_90-17-1.pdf|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150108125912/http://www.history.army.mil/html/books/090/90-17-1/CMH_Pub_90-17-1.pdf|url-status=dead|archive-date=January 8, 2015}}{{PD-notice}}</ref>{{rp|197–8}}

; 17 January
300 ARVN [[Republic of Vietnam Airborne Division|paratroopers]] with U.S. air support and advisers raided a suspected camp holding American [[prisoners of war]] in Cambodia. No POWs were in the camp, but 30 PAVN soldiers were captured.<ref name=Bowman>{{cite book|last=Bowman|first=John|title=The World Almanac of the Vietnam War|publisher=Pharos Books|year=1985|isbn=978-0886872724}}</ref>{{rp|275}}

Joint U.S., South Vietnamese and Cambodian convoys begin up the [[Mekong River]] to supply Phnom Penh after [[Khmer Rouge]] attacks had cut off overland supply from Cambodia's seaports.<ref name=Mercogliano>{{cite book|last=Mercogliano|first=Salvatore|title=Fourth Arm of Defense Sealift and Maritime Logistics in the Vietnam war|publisher=Naval History and Heritage Command|year=2017|isbn=9780945274964|url=https://www.history.navy.mil/content/dam/nhhc/research/publications/publication-508-pdf/NHHC4thArmDefense_final_508.pdf}}{{PD-notice}}</ref>{{rp|58}}

;21-2 January
PAVN sappers attacked [[Pochentong Airport|Pochentong Airfield]] near Phnom Penh and destroyed or damaged 69 [[Khmer Air Force|Khmer National Aviation]] (AVNK) aircraft and killed 39 AVNK personnel, effectively destroying the AVNK.<ref>{{cite book|last=Conboy|first=Kenneth|title=FANK: A History of the Cambodian Armed Forces, 1970-1975|publisher=Equinox Publishing (Asia) Pte Ltd|year=2011|isbn=9789793780863|pages=217–8}}</ref>

;24 January
[[NBC]] reported that soldiers of the [[173rd Airborne Brigade]] stationed at [[Landing Zone English]] were buying [[heroin]] from a Vietnamese house on the base and the South Vietnamese then proceeded to demolish the house.<ref name=Hammond>{{cite book|last=Hammond|first=William|title=The U.S. Army in Vietnam Public Affairs The Military and the Media 1968-1973|publisher=U.S. Army Center of Military History|year=1996|url=https://history.army.mil/html/books/091/91-2/index.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120913060001/http://www.history.army.mil/html/books/091/91-2/index.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=September 13, 2012|isbn=978-0160486968}}{{PD-notice}}</ref>{{rp|393}}

;30 January
U.S. forces launch Operation Dewey Canyon II to reopen [[National Route 9 (Vietnam)|Route 9]] to the abandoned [[Khe Sanh Combat Base]] as the initial phase of Operation Lam Son 719.<ref name=USMC/>{{rp|447}}

==February==
;2 February - 30 April
[[Campaign 74B]] was a PAVN combined arms operation that recaptured the strategic [[Plain of Jars]] and brought the PAVN [[316th Division (Vietnam)|316th Division]] within artillery range of the major RLA base at [[Long Tieng]]. The assault was stopped by Thai mercenary forces and U.S. air support and the PAVN withdrew as they exhausted their supplies.<ref name=Conboy/>{{rp|295–300}}

;3 February - 10 March
In Operation Hoang Dien 103, units of [[III Marine Expeditionary Force|III Marine Amphibious Force]], [[Republic of Korea Marine Corps]] [[2nd Marine Division (South Korea)|2nd Marine Brigade]], ARVN 51st Regiment, 146th [[South Vietnamese Popular Force|Popular Force]] (PF) Platoon, 39th [[South Vietnamese Regional Force|Regional Force]] (RF) Company and [[People's Self-Defense Force]] (PSDF) units combed the Da Nang lowlands and lowland fringes, killing 330 PAVN/VC, while losing 46 killed, including two Americans.<ref name=Cosmas/>{{rp|447}}

;8 February
[[List of heads of state of Cambodia|President of the Khmer Republic]] (Cambodia) [[Lon Nol]] suffered a [[stroke]] and passed control to Prince [[Sisowath Sirik Matak]].<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|362}}

[[File:Map Lam Son 719.jpg|thumb|South Vietnam and Operation Lam Son 719.]]
;8 February- 25 March
Operation Lam Son 719 ({{langx|vi|Chiến dịch Lam Sơn 719 or Chiến dịch đường 9 &ndash; Nam Lào}}) was an invasion by 20,000 soldiers of the armed forces of South Vietnam of southeastern Laos. The objective of the operation was the disruption of the Ho Chi Minh Trail which supplied PAVN and VC forces in South Vietnam. Although claiming victory, the ARVN withdrew from Laos in disorder and suffered 9,000 casualties. U.S. forces were prohibited from entering Laos but provided air support for the operation and had 253 soldiers killed and many helicopters destroyed.<ref name=Fulghum>{{cite book|last1=Fulghum|first1=David|last2=Maitland|first2=Terrence|title=The Vietnam Experience South Vietnam on Trial: Mid-1970–1972|publisher=Boston Publishing Company|year=1984|isbn=0939526107|page=61}}</ref>{{rp|70–90}}

;10 February
In Operation Lam Son 719, an armoured column of the ARVN reached Ban Dong, 20 kilometers inside Laos and one half the distance to [[Tchepone]], the objective of the invasion. The route, Highway 9, was only barely passable and the advance stalled. The PAVN concentrated their resistance against a number of small bases established in Laos to support the operation.<ref name=Fulghum/>{{rp|75}}

A [[Republic of Vietnam Air Force]] (RVNAF) [[Bell UH-1 Iroquois|UH-1]] helicopter carrying photojournalists [[Larry Burrows]], [[Henri Huet]], Kent Potter and Keizaburo Shimamoto and seven others was shot down over Laos killing all on board.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.vanityfair.com/magazine/1999/12/burrows199912|title=Saigon Quartet|author=Richard Pyle|magazine=Vanity Fair|access-date=9 July 2020}}</ref>

;12 February
[[Armée Nationale Khmère]] (ANK) Brigadier General Neak Sam was killed along with 14 of his men during operations against the PAVN off Highway 2 {{convert|10|mi}} south of [[Doun Kaev (town)|Takéo]].<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Cambodian General killed, first of war|date=13 February 1971|page=2}}</ref>

;13 February
Lon Nol was flown to [[Hawaii]] aboard a [[United States Air Force]] (USAF) jet and taken to [[Tripler Army Medical Center]] for treatment.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Ailing Cambodia Premier flies to Honolulu for convalescence|author=Iver Peterson|date=14 February 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;15-17 February
U.S. jets hit North Vietnamese SAM sites around [[Vinh]] over three consecutive days in "protective reaction" strikes.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Missile sites in North Vietnam attacked by U.S. 3d day in row|author=Ralph Blumenthal|date=17 February 1971|page=5}}</ref>

;16 February
USAF Colonel Gerald V. Kehrli, the highest-ranking U.S. officer court-martialled in South Vietnam, was found guilty of seven [[marijuana]] offenses and sentenced to three years in prison and a $15,000 fine.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=A Colonel Is Given 3-Year Drug Term|date=17 February 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;16 February - 3 April
[[Operation Desert Rat]] was an RLA operation intended to harass the PAVN as they fought off the ARVN in Operation Lam Son 719. The operation resulted in 121 PAVN killed and 39 trucks destroyed.<ref name=Conboy/>{{rp|290–1}}

;17 February
In his first press conference since the start of Lam Son 719, Nixon refused to put any limits on the use of U.S. airpower in Indochina other on than the use of tactical nuclear weapons. Nixon warned of stiffening PAVN resistance in Laos as the PAVN sought to defend their supply lines into South Vietnam and fuelled speculation that the South might attack into North Vietnam.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Nixon refuses to rule out wider air role in the war or a Saigon push to North|author=Robert B. Semple Jr.|date=18 February 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;18 February
North Vietnam said that the U.S. was threatening to expand the war into North Vietnam and that this also threatened [[China]].<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Hanoi says moves by U.S. pose threat to Red China|date=19 February 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;20 February
ARVN forces abandoned Landing Zone (LZ) Ranger North ({{Coord|16.744|N|106.493|E|display=inline}}) in Laos after two days of artillery bombardment and then armor-infantry assaults. ARVN losses were 178 dead or missing while an estimated 639 PAVN troops were killed during the battle.<ref name=Hinh>{{cite book|last=Nguyen|first=Duy Hinh|title=Operation Lam Sơn 719|publisher=United States Army Center of Military History|year=1979|isbn=978-1984054463}}{{PD-notice}}</ref>{{rp|79}}

;22 February
A story in ''The New York Times'' titled "Saigon's Rangers driven from an outpost in Laos" depicted the fight at Ranger North as a panicked debacle for the ARVN with able-bodied Rangers pushing their way onto medevac helicopters.<ref name=Hammond/>{{rp|445–6}}

;23 February
New ARVN [[I Corps (South Vietnam)|I Corps]] commander Lieutenant General [[Đỗ Cao Trí]] died in a helicopter crash near [[Bien Hoa Air Base]]. Photojournalist [[François Sully]] leapt {{convert|75|ft}} from the burning helicopter but later died of his injuries.<ref name=Fulghum/>{{rp|61}}

;25 February
ARVN Airborne forces abandoned Fire Support Base (FSB) 31 ({{Coord|16.715|N|106.426|E|display=inline}}) in Laos after two days of artillery bombardment and then armor-infantry assaults. PAVN losses were approximately 250 killed and 11 [[PT-76]] and [[T-54]] tanks destroyed. The Airborne had 155 killed and over 100 captured.<ref name=Fulghum/>{{rp|85}} 

;28 February
The U.S. moved armored units to the Laos border to prevent any PAVN armored thrust eastwards after the loss of FSB 31.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. moves tanks to seal frontier to enemy in Laos|author=Craig R. Whitney|date=1 March 1971|page=1}}</ref>

==March==
; 1 March
A bomb exploded in the [[United States Capitol]] building at 1:32&nbsp;a.m., injuring nobody but causing $300,000 in damage. The [[Weather Underground]] took credit for the bombing which was in protest of the invasion of Laos.<ref>{{cite web|date=March 1, 1971|title=This Day in History {{!}} March 01 {{!}} Bomb explodes in Capitol building|url=http://www.history.com/this-day-in-history/bomb-explodes-in-capitol-building|website=www.history.com|accessdate= 12 Aug 2015}}</ref>

;2 March
The ARVN abandoned FSB 30 ({{Coord|16.696|N|106.486|E|display=inline}}) in Laos after heavy PAVN artillery fire and ground assaults.<ref name=Hinh/>{{rp|92}}

A PAVN mortar and sapper attack on Cambodia's only oil refinery at [[Kompong Som]] killed five Cambodians and destroyed 80% of the facility, four PAVN were killed.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|365}}

Eleven ARVN were killed in an ambush near [[Suong]], Cambodia.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Ambush by foe kills 11|date=3 March 1971|page=4}}</ref>

;3 March
The [[5th Special Forces Group (United States)|5th Special Forces Group]] departed South Vietnam.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|365}}

;4 March
North Vietnam fired three [[S-75 Dvina|SA-2]] missiles at U.S. jets flying over northern South Vietnam.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Foe said to fire across the DMZ at U.S. aircraft|author=Alvin Shuster|date=4 March 1971|page=1}}</ref>

A U.S. Army [[Beechcraft U-21 Ute|U-21]] on a radio research mission was shot down by a SAM near the DMZ killing all five crewmen on board.<ref name=U-21>{{cite web|url=https://www.baaa-acro.com/aircraft/beechcraft-u-21-ute?page=1|title=Beechcraft U-21 Ute|publisher=Bureau of Aircraft Accidents Archives|accessdate=6 December 2021}}</ref>

In a press conference Nixon said that Operation Lam Son 719 had seriously damaged North Vietnam's military capabilities and would aid the U.S. withdrawal of forces.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=President says Laos operation aids U.S. pullout|author=Robert B. Semple Jr.|date=5 March 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;5 March
The [[11th Armored Cavalry Regiment]], less its 2nd Squadron departed South Vietnam.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|365}}

;6 March
In the largest airborne assault of the war, 276 UH-1 helicopters transported two ARVN battalions to capture Tchepone without major resistance.<ref name=Fulghum/>{{rp|85}}

;9 March
President [[Nguyễn Văn Thiệu]] of South Vietnam ordered the withdrawal of South Vietnamese troops from Laos. He ignored the recommendation of Abrams that South Vietnam reinforce its troops in Laos and hold its position. The withdrawal became a rout with South Vietnam suffering heavy casualties.<ref name=Fulghum/>{{rp|86–90}}

U.S. jets and helicopter gunships accidentally attacked ARVN forces near Tchepone killing nine.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. bombing errors|date=10 March 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;10 March
Following a visit to Hanoi, Chinese Premier [[Zhou Enlai]] issued a communique pledging Chinese support for the North Vietnamese in its fight against the U.S.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|365}}

[[Australia]]n [[Minister for Defence (Australia)|Defense Minister]] [[Malcolm Fraser]] resigned claiming that [[Prime Minister of Australia|Prime Minister]] [[John Gorton]] was interfering with his ministerial responsibilities and that Gorton was "not fit to hold the great office of Prime Minister." Gorton then resigned as Prime Minister and was replaced by [[William McMahon]].<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|366}}

FANK High Command claimed that 51,505 PAVN/VC had been killed in fighting over the previous year in Cambodia while FANK losses were 4,496 killed. Sources said the FANK losses were largely accurate while the PAVN/VC losses were highly exaggerated.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Cambodia reports losses|date=11 March 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;10 March to 1 July
The [[198th Infantry Brigade (United States)|198th Light Infantry Brigade]] launched [[Operation Finney Hill]] to secure lines of communication and pacification operations in the coastal area of Quảng Ngãi Province. The operation resulted in 454 PAVN/VC killed and eight captured, U.S. losses were 32 killed.<ref name=ORLLNOV71>{{cite web|url=https://www.vietnam.ttu.edu/reports/images.php?img=/images/1387/1387ORLL23111971.pdf|title=Operational Report - Lessons Learned, 23rd Infantry Division, (Americal), Period ending 15 October 1971|publisher=Department of the Army|date=1 November 1971|accessdate=28 June 2020}}{{PD-notice}}</ref>{{rp|29}}

;10 March to 1 July
The [[196th Infantry Brigade (United States)|196th Light Infantry Brigade]] launched [[Operation Middlesex Peak]] a security operation to prevent PAVN/VC infiltration into the coastal lowlands of Quảng Tín and Quảng Ngãi Provinces. The operation resulted in 463 PAVN/VC killed and 22 captured and U.S. losses of 50 killed.<ref name=ORLLNOV71/>{{rp|26}}

;13 March
Hanoi described the assault on Tchepone as a "seven act farce".<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Hanoi calls Tchepone drive a seven-act farce|date=14 March 1971|page=14}}</ref>

;14 March
Two USMC [[Douglas A-4 Skyhawk|A-4 Skyhawks]] accidentally bombed an ARVN 1st Division position in Laos killing 9 ARVN.<ref name=Checo>{{cite book|title=Lam Son 719 30 January – 24 March 1971 The South Vietnamese incursion into Laos|publisher=Headquarters Pacific Air Force Project CHECO|date=24 March 1971|url=https://apps.dtic.mil/sti/pdfs/ADA486864.pdf|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211123054920/https://apps.dtic.mil/sti/pdfs/ADA486864.pdf|url-status=live|archive-date=November 23, 2021}}{{PD-notice}}</ref>{{rp|121}}

; 15 March
[[File:Mortar impacts near two 1st Marine Aircraft Wing AH-1G Cobra helicopters from Marine Light Helicopter Squadron 367, Khe Sanh Combat Base.tif|thumb|right|PAVN fire hits near Khe Sanh Combat Base]]
PAVN artillery began to shell Khe Sanh Combat Base, the main base supporting Operation Lam Son 719.<ref name=Fulghum/>{{rp|92}}

''[[Newsweek]]'' magazine's cover story was titled "The Helicopter War" and described the intensity of U.S. helicopter operations and PAVN antiaircraft fire over Laos.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Just Say It Was the Comancheros|magazine=Newsweek|author=Kevin Buckley|date=15 March 1971}}</ref>

;17 March
MACV advised Washington that budget and manpower restraints were driving the U.S. withdrawal more than the progress of Vietnamization and PAVN/VC battlefield actions.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Funds said to be key to G.I. pullout rate|author=Iver Peterson|date=18 March 1971|page=14}}</ref>

;21 March
Two platoons from Bravo Company, 1st Squadron, [[1st Cavalry Regiment (United States)|1st Cavalry Regiment]] refused orders to retrieve a downed helicopter and damaged vehicles on Route 9 between [[Lang Vei]] and the Laos border. The unit was withdrawn and the mission completed by another unit and the captain was relieved of command.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=2 G.I. platoons near Laos refuse orders to advance|date=22 March 1971|page=1}}</ref>

The Nixon Administration claimed that the South Vietnamese withdrawal from Laos was an "orderly retreat" and not a rout.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Nixon aides use Agnew phrase of "Orderly retreat"|author=Tad Szulc|date=22 March 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;21-3 March
The PAVN fired rockets at [[Luang Prabang Airport]] killing six civilians and damaging five aircraft and carried out sapper attacks on six RLA outposts around the airport.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. families fly out after Laos airport raid|date=22 March 1971|page=4}}</ref>

;22-3 March
U.S. jets attacked North Vietnamese SAM and antiaircraft sites between the DMZ and the 19th parallel, losing one [[McDonnell Douglas F-4 Phantom II|F-4 Phantom II]] shot down.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|366}}

;23 March
Kissinger admitted to Nixon that Lam Son 719 "comes out as clearly not a success". The failure of Lam Son 719 was called by one scholar "the military turning point of the war".<ref name=Asselin>{{cite book|last=Asselin|first=Pierre|title=A Bitter Peace: Washington, Hanoi, and the Making of the Paris Agreement|publisher=University of North Carolina Press|year=2002|isbn=0-807854174}}</ref>{{rp|28}}

[[NBC News]] reported the evacuation of an ARVN regiment on Route 9 where soldiers clung to the skids of helicopters rather than waiting for more helicopters that might not come.<ref name=Hammond/>{{rp|477}}

A PAVN sapper attack on Khe Sanh Combat Base resulted in three Americans killed and several aircraft and two ammunition dumps destroyed, PAVN losses were 14 killed and one captured.<ref name=Fulghum/>{{rp|96}}

The Pentagon admitted that Operation Lam Son 719 had been cut short due to the strong PAVN response.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Push cut short, Pentagon admits|author=Tad Szulc|date=22 March 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;24 March
U.S. air cavalry sighted PAVN armored vehicles at five different locations near Route 9 within {{convert|1|mi}} of the Laotian side of the border. Helicopter gunships and tactical airstrikes destroyed ten PT-76s.<ref name=Hinh/>{{rp|120}}

; 25 March
In Operation Lam Son 719, most South Vietnamese soldiers had crossed the border back into South Vietnam and fighting in Laos ceased.<ref name=Fulghum/>{{rp|90}} ''The New York Times'' reported that Pentagon sources said that Thiệu had limited the offensive to reduce South Vietnamese losses.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Thieu said to have curbed offensive to reduce loss|author=William Beecher|date=25 March 1971|page=1}}</ref>

The North Vietnamese and VC delegates to the Paris Peace Talks boycotted the session in protest at the recent U.S. bombing of North Vietnam.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|367}}

;27 March
Three Americans were killed in PAVN shelling of Khe Sanh Combat Base.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Foe attacks G.I.s near Laos border|author=Alvin Shuster|date=28 March 1971|page=1}}</ref> One American was killed by PAVN artillery fire at Lang Vei and three Americans from the 1st Brigade, [[5th Infantry Division (United States)|5th Infantry Division (Mechanized)]] were killed in a clash with PAVN forces north of Route 9.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Clashes near border|date=28 March 1971|page=9}}</ref>

''The New York Times'' reported that the morale of South Vietnamese units that had fought in Laos had been shattered by the fighting there.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Spirit of Saigon's army shaken in loss|author=Gloria Emerson|date=28 March 1971|page=1}}</ref>

; 28 March
Several dozen PAVN [[sappers]] infiltrated [[Battle of FSB Mary Ann|Fire Support Base Mary Ann]] in [[Quảng Tín Province]] and killed 30 American soldiers. Mary Ann was scheduled to be turned over to the ARVN and the U.S. forces withdrawn. Several American officers were demoted or reprimanded for "substandard performance".<ref name=Fulghum/>{{rp|7–9}}

;29 March 
The jury at a military [[court-martial]] convicted Lieutenant [[William Calley]] of the premeditated murder of 22 Vietnamese civilians during the [[My Lai massacre]] of 1968. Calley was the only soldier convicted for his role in the massacre.<ref name="upi.com">[http://www.upi.com/Audio/Year_in_Review/Events-of-1971/Calley-Trial%2C-Foreign-Affairs/12295509436546-8/ - 1971 Year in Review: Calley Trial, Foreign Affairs]</ref>

PAVN/VC forces killed 103 South Vietnamese civilians and destroyed 1,500 homes in the [[Duc Duc massacre]] in Duc Duc District, [[Quảng Nam Province]].<ref name=USMC/>{{rp|231–2}}

;29-30 March
The PAVN captured a {{convert|10|mi}} stretch of Route 4 in Cambodia.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|367}}

;30 March
A confidential U.S. Army directive ordered the interception and confiscation of anti-Vietnam War and other dissident material being sent to U.S. military personnel in South Vietnam.<ref name=15thFA>{{cite web|url=http://www.landscaper.net/timelin.htm|title=Vietnam war Timeline|publisher=15th Field Artillery Regiment|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070416122046/http://www.landscaper.net/timelin.htm|archivedate=2007-04-16|accessdate=10 August 2020}}</ref>

; 31 March 
Calley was sentenced to life imprisonment and hard labor at [[Fort Leavenworth]] for his role in the My Lai massacre.<ref name="upi.com"/> [[The Battle Hymn of Lt. Calley]] was released by [[Terry Nelson (musician)|Terry Nelson]] around this date protesting Calley's innocence.<ref>{{cite web|last=Brummer |first=Justin|title=The Vietnam War: A History in Song |url=https://www.historytoday.com/miscellanies/vietnam-war-history-song|website=History Today|access-date=1 October 2021}}</ref>

At [[Đông Hà Combat Base]], Thiệu addressed the survivors of the incursion and claimed that the operation in Laos was "the biggest victory ever".<ref name=Tilford>{{cite book|last=Tilford|first=Earl|title=Setup: What the Air Force Did in Vietnam and Why|publisher=Air University Press|year=1991|page=203}}</ref>

House Democrats voted 138:62 to call for an end to U.S. involvement in Indochina by the start of 1973.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=House Democrats ask pullout by '73|author=Marjorie Hunter|date=1 April 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;31 March to 2 April
Following B-52 strikes and tactical airstrikes on the PAVN Base Area 611 in the Laotian Salient, the ARVN ''Hac Bao'' Reconnaissance Company of the [[1st Division (South Vietnam)|1st Division]] supported by the 2nd Squadron, [[17th Cavalry Regiment (United States)|17th Air Cavalry]] was landed in the area. The ''Hac Bao'' found 85 PAVN dead and 18 destroyed weapons. That night vehicles were heard moving to the south and airstrikes destroyed five vehicles. On the morning of 1 April the ''Hac Bao'' found a destroyed fuel dump and tunnel complex housing armored vehicles before being extracted on the afternoon of 2 April.<ref name=Hinh/>{{rp|122–4}}

;31 March to 16 April
The PAVN 66th Regiment attacked [[Firebase 6]] overrunning the base. Lieutenant [[Brian Thacker]] team leader of an Integrated Observation System would be awarded the Medal of Honor for his actions during the battle. ARVN reinforcements were flown in and recaptured the base although PAVN units remained in force in the surrounding area. More than 280 PAVN were killed, mostly by airstrikes.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|367–8}}<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Recapture of base overrun by enemy reported by Saigon|date=2 April 1971|page=1}}</ref> The PAVN attacked the base again on 3 April with the ARVN reporting a further 900 PAVN killed and a total of 68 ARVN killed, though sources in Saigon expressed skepticism over these figures.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Enemy attacks base for 4th day|date=4 April 1971|page=1}}</ref> By 11 April approximately 1,000 ARVN were defending the base, opposed by an estimated 7,000 PAVN.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=A major battle seen developing near Laos border|date=12 April 1971|page=1}}</ref> The PAVN were pounded with B-52 strikes and [[BLU-82]] [[Fuel Air Explosive]] bombs.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Enemy is pounded by 7 1/2 ton bombs|date=13 April 1971|page=1}}</ref> On 14 April a ground relief force reached the base encountering minimal opposition to lift the siege.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Besieged outpost gets some relief|date=14 April 1971|page=1}}</ref> On 15 April in a skirmish {{convert|1.5|mi}} southeast of the base the ARVN killed 38 PAVN for the loss of four killed.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon's troops open a drive on enemy regiment in highlands|date=14 April 1971|page=2}}</ref>

==April==
;3 April
Nixon ordered Calley to be transferred from Fort Leavenworth prison to house arrest.<ref name=Bowman/>{{rp|274}}

;6 April
The ''Hac Bao'' conducted a further raid into the Laotian Salient finding 15 PAVN dead and a destroyed tunnel complex, during the operation USAF airstrikes destroyed three anti-aircraft gun positions. This raid marked the official end of Operation Lam Son 719.<ref name=Hinh/>{{rp|124–5}}

;7 April
Khe Sanh Combat Base, reactivated to support Operation Lam Son 719, was abandoned once again.<ref name=Fulghum/>{{rp|96}}

In a press conference, Nixon claimed that "Tonight I can report that Vietnamization has succeeded." and announced the withdrawal of a further 100,000 troops from South Vietnam between May and November 1971.<ref name=Hammond/>{{rp|484}} Senior military planners said that the withdrawals would make 1972 a risky year for U.S. forces as the changing military balance could tempt Hanoi to mount a major offensive to demoralize or topple the South Vietnamese government and undermine the Nixon Administration in a U.S. presidential election year.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Planners see risks for '72 in Vietnam|author=William Beecher|date=8 April 1971|page=7}}</ref>

The [[USA Table Tennis|U.S. Table Tennis Association]] announced that it had accepted a Chinese invitation to send a team to make a ten-day tour of China following the [[1971 World Table Tennis Championships|world championships in Nagoya, Japan]]. This marked the beginning of the U.S.-China [[Ping-pong diplomacy]].<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. table tennis team to visit China for week|author=Takashi Oka|date=8 April 1971|page=1}}</ref>

[[United Press International|UPI]] correspondent [[Kate Webb]], Japanese photojournalist Toshiichi Suzuki and Cambodian journalists Tea Kim Heang, Chhim Sarath, Vorn and Charoon were captured by PAVN troops fighting ANK forces on Highway 4.<ref name=Becker>{{cite book|last=Becker|first=Elizabeth|title=You Don't Belong Here How Three Women Rewrote the Story of War|publisher=Public Affairs Books|year=2021|isbn=9781541768208}}</ref>{{rp|189}}

;7 April to 5 June
[[Operation Xieng Dong]] was a successful RLA operation to defend the capital Luang Prabang against a PAVN attack. RLA forces from across the country converged on the capital and forced the PAVN 335th Regiment to withdraw.<ref name=Conboy/>{{rp|293–4}}

;8-10 April
The PAVN attacked Firebase Lonely ({{Coord|13.44|N|108.089|E|display=inline}}) in the [[Central Highlands (Vietnam)|Central Highlands]], losing 68 killed.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=2 South Vietnamese bases are reported under heavy attack|date=8 April 1971|page=9}}</ref> They then laid siege to the base losing a further 25 killed for the loss of three ARVN.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Besieged fire base fights off attack by Hanoi commandos|date=10 April 1971|page=7}}</ref>

;8 April to 11 July
Operation Montana Mustang was a 1st Brigade, 5th Infantry Division (Mechanized) operation intended to locate and destroy enemy forces, eliminate VC infrastructure, conduct reaction/exploitation operations and assist in pacification and Vietnamization in Quảng Trị Province. The operation resulted in 91 PAVN and 57 U.S. killed.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://oai.dtic.mil/oai/oai?&verb=getRecord&metadataPrefix=html&identifier=AD0530751|title=Lessons Learned, 1st Inf Bde, 5th Inf Div (Mech), RCS CSFOR - 65 (R3)|date=19 Aug 1971|author=Adjutant General's Office (Army)|access-date=23 July 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110823130052/http://oai.dtic.mil/oai/oai?&verb=getRecord&metadataPrefix=html&identifier=AD0530751|archive-date=23 August 2011|url-status=dead}}</ref>

;14 April
The [[3rd Marine Amphibious Brigade]] was activated at [[Red Beach Base Area|Camp Jay K. Brooks]] and [[III Marine Amphibious Force]] transferred all remaining Marine forces to it.<ref name=USMC/>{{rp|238}}

Nixon announced a relaxation of the 20 year old trade embargo with China, allowing the export of non-strategic goods.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Nixon eases trade embargo to allow non-strategic exports: Chou says "new page" has opened|date=15 April 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;16 April to October
Elements of the ARVN 1st Division commanded by Brigadier General [[Vũ Văn Giai]] and a battalion from the U.S. [[101st Airborne Division#Vietnam War|101st Airborne Division (Airmobile)]] began Operation Lam Son 720 against PAVN bases in the [[A Sầu Valley]] meeting minimal resistance.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|368}} Abrams stated that a new South Vietnamese incursion into Laos could not be ruled out.<ref name=NYT18Apr>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=A new incursion in Laos possible, Abrams indicates|author=Craig R. Whitney|date=18 April 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;17 April
The South Vietnamese held a victory parade and award ceremony for participants in Lam Son 719 outside the [[Imperial City of Huế|Huế Citadel]]. Thiệu repeated his "Four nos": no coalition, no neutralization, no territorial concessions and no Communists operating openly in South Vietnam.<ref name=NYT18Apr/>

;18 April
Kỳ stated that it would take 15 to 20 years before South Vietnam was able to defend itself, but that the pace of the U.S. withdrawal was reasonable.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Ky sees long road to self-defense|date=19 April 1971|page=7}}</ref>

;20 April
Lon Nol resigned as President of the Khmer Republic, however he would rescind the resignation a week later and stay on as figurehead leader with Sisowath Sirk Matak exercising power.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|362}}

The Pentagon stated that 209 [[Fragging]] incidents had occurred in South Vietnam in 1970, up from 96 in 1969.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|369}}

;21 April
Testifying before the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, Republican Representative [[Pete McCloskey]] said that the Nixon Administration had hidden the extent of U.S. bombing in northern Laos. Democratic Senator [[Edward Kennedy]] described the unrestricted air war there as a "bloodbath".<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=McClosky says U.S. hid extent of Laos bombings|date=22 April 1971|page=3}}</ref>

''The New York Times'' published an article on refugees in Indochina which it said numbered five million in South Vietnam, one million in Cambodia and 750,000 in Laos.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Hopes thin for millions adrift across Indochina|author=Henry Kamm|date=21 April 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;22 April
[[John Kerry]] of [[Vietnam Veterans Against the War]] testified before the Senate Foreign Relations Committee stating: "Someone has to die so that President Nixon won't be - and these are his words - 'the first president to lose a war.' How do you ask a man to be the last man to die in Vietnam? How do you ask a man to be the last man to die for a mistake?"<ref>{{cite book|last1=Ward|first1=Geoffrey|last2=Burns|first2=Ken|title=The Vietnam War An Intimate History|publisher=Alfred A. Knopf|year=2017|isbn=978-0307700254|page=480}}</ref>

;23-4 April
Members of Vietnam Veterans Against the War threw away over 700 medals on the west steps of the Capitol building in Washington to protest the war in what they called [[Vietnam Veterans Against the War#Dewey Canyon III – Washington, D.C., April 1971|Operation Dewey Canyon III]].<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Veterans Discard Medals In War Protest At Capitol|date=24 April 1971|page=1}}</ref> The next day, antiwar organizers claimed that 500,000 marched, making this the largest demonstration since the November 1969 march, while officials claimed the number was 200,000.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Reports of Its Death Have Been Greatly Exaggerated|author=James Buckley|date=25 April 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;24 April
Seven soldiers from the [[11th Infantry Brigade (United States)|11th Infantry Brigade]] were killed by a VC booby-trap while on patrol {{convert|26|mi}} southeast of Quảng Ngai.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Booby-trap kills 7 G.I.s|date=25 April 1971|page=4}}</ref>

;26 April
The PAVN attacked the ARVN base at Hoang Anh {{convert|15|mi}} northwest of Quảng Ngai killing 22 ARVN, while losing 21 PAVN. Six Americans were killed in two PAVN/VC attacks on a land-clearing unit {{convert|20|mi}} northwest of Saigon.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Several attacks by foe reported in South Vietnam|date=26 April 1971|page=1}}</ref> A PAVN mortar attack on an ammunition dump near Quy Nhon caused a huge explosion and killed ten ARVN and two civilians and wounded a further 18 civilians. PF forces killed 42 PAVN in clashes {{convert|30|mi}} south of Da Nang.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Munition dump destroyed as foe steps up attacks|date=27 April 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;27 April
The Senate Armed Service Committee unveiled proposed legislation limiting the annual draft to 150,000 men for the next two years, more than the Nixon Administration's current draft plans of 10,000 per month.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Senate units asks ceiling on draft for next 2 years|date=28 April 1971|page=1}}</ref>

The South Vietnamese Government ordered Don Luce to leave the country by 16 May. This was in retaliation for his role in revealing the "Tiger cages" on [[Côn Sơn Island]] in 1970.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon orders ouster of writer who reported on "Tiger cages"|author=Gloria Emerson|date=28 April 1971|page=15}}</ref>

;29 April
ANK troops killed 57 PAVN for the loss of 12 killed in an action to relieve ANK forces besieged on the Pich Nil Pass ({{Coord|11.195|N|104.074|E|display=inline, title}}) on Route 4.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Cambodian drive nearing key pass|date=29 April 1971|page=7}}</ref>

The U.S. [[1st Cavalry Division (United States)|1st Cavalry Division (Airmobile)]] less its [[3rd Brigade Combat Team, 1st Cavalry Division|3rd Brigade]] departed South Vietnam.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|370}}

;29 April to 1 July
The 196th Light Infantry Brigade launched [[Operation Caroline Hill]] to locate and engage PAVN/VC forces, lines of communications and base areas and provide security for pacification programs in the area west and south of Da Nang following the departure of the III Marine Amphibious Force. The operation resulted in 161 PAVN/VC killed and 11 captured and U.S. losses of 15 killed.<ref name=ORLLNOV71/>{{rp|34}}

; 30 April
Nixon welcomed the [[1st Marine Division]] back from South Vietnam at a ceremony at [[Camp Pendleton]].<ref name=USMC/>{{rp|242}}

[[I Field Force, Vietnam]] was disestablished and its assets formed the basis for its successor, the Second Regional Assistance Command (SRAC).<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|370}}

[[American Forces Network|American Forces Vietnam Network]] was instructed by MACV to stop playing The Battle Hymn of Lt. Calley.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. command in Vietnam bars "Battle Hymn of Calley" from radio network, citing pending appeal|author=Alvin Shuster|date=1 May 1971|page=4}}</ref>

Five Americans were killed when UH-1H #68-16303 was shot down in Operation Lam Son 720 in the A Sầu Valley.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=B-52s attack ambush area in A Shau|date=2 May 1971|page=14}}</ref>

Medal of Honor recipient [[Dwight H. Johnson]] was shot dead as he attempted to rob a convenience store in [[Detroit]].<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=From Dak To to Detroit: Death of a troubled hero|author=Jon Nordheimer|date=26 May 1971|page=1}}</ref>

==May==
;1 May
UPI correspondent Kate Webb, missing since 7 April and presumed dead, was released from PAVN captivity in Kampong Speu, Cambodia.<ref name=Becker/>{{rp|190–7}}

;2 May
[[II Field Force, Vietnam]] was disestablished with its assets providing the basis for its successor, Third Regional Assistance Command (TRAC).<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|370}}

;2-4 May
A reinforced company of VC infantry and sappers stormed [[Đại Lộc District]] Headquarters behind a mortar and rocket barrage. RF/PF forces killed 95 VC and captured 43 individual and crew-served weapons, at a cost of 15 dead and 43 wounded.<ref name=USMC/>{{rp|243}}

; 3 May
15,000 soldiers and police arrested more than 7,000 persons in the [[1971 May Day Protests]] in Washington D.C.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|370}}

The State Department acknowledged that B-52s had been bombing targets in northern Laos for the past two years.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. acknowledges use of B-52s for raids over northern Laos|author=John W. Finney|date=3 May 1971|page=10}}</ref>

;4 May
North Vietnam effectively rejected a U.S. proposal that U.S. prisoners of war be held in Sweden until a peace settlement was agreed.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Hanoi cool to plan of U.S. on prisoners|date=5 May 1971|page=11}}</ref>

; 5 May 
1,146 protesters against the war were arrested on the U.S. Capitol grounds trying to shut down the U.S. Congress. This brought the total arrested during 1971 May Day Protests to over 12,000.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Protesters Fail to Stop Congress, Police Seize 1,146|author=James M. McNaughton|date=6 May 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;6 May
A PAVN artillery attack on Da Nang killed six civilians and destroyed three houses.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Enemy shells Danang|date=6 May 1971|page=2}}</ref>

An ARVN mechanized force killed 72 PAVN in fighting near Snuol, Cambodia and U.S. helicopter gunships killed five PAVN {{convert|52|mi}} southeast of Phnom Penh.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon reports new clash in Cambodia|date=7 May 1971|page=3}}</ref>

U.S. negotiators at the Paris Peace Talks said that there could be no discussion of a full withdrawal of U.S. forces unless North Vietnamese forces were also withdrawn. The North Vietnamese continued to deny that it had any troops in South Vietnam, Cambodia or Laos.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. bids Hanoi discuss its own pullout|date=7 May 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;8-9 May
A 24-hour truce to mark Buddha's birthday saw 51 ceasefire violations resulting in 43 civilians, 19 PAVN/VC, 11 ARVN and two U.S. killed. 36 of the civilians were killed when a ferry hit a mine in Quảng Trị Province.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=51 ceasefire violations charged to foe by Allies|author=Ivor Peterson|date=10 May 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;11 May
It was reported that FANK artillery had caused severe damage to the [[Angkor Wat]] temple complex and killed ten civilians there three months previously. The area had been occupied by the PAVN since June 1970.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Angkor Wat reported damaged by Cambodian shells|date=12 May 1971|page=1}}</ref>

Democratic Senator [[John C. Stennis]] introduced war powers legislation to limit the power of the President to commit the U.S. to war without congressional approval.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Stennis seeks war curb on President|author=John W. Finney|date=12 May 1971|page=14}}</ref>

;11-15 May
A 5,000 strong ARVN force conducted operations in Kandol Chrum and Kompong Trach, Cambodia killing at least 60 PAVN.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon force reported making new sweep in east Cambodia|date=15 May 1971|page=5}}</ref>

;12 May
U.S. jets destroyed 13 PAVN antiaircraft guns around the [[Mụ Giạ Pass]].<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. says planes hit guns in North|date=13 May 1971|page=11}}</ref>

In Operation Lam Son 720 the PAVN attacked a South Vietnamese Marine battalion twice, losing more than 200 killed while ARVN casualties were 90 killed and wounded.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Marines in Ashau report 2 attacks|date=14 May 1971|page=9}}</ref>

;13 May
The Paris Peace Talks between North Vietnam, South Vietnam, the VC and the U.S. entered their fourth year. Little or no progress had been made.<ref name=Bowman/>{{rp|283}}

;15 May
MACV acknowledged that heroin addiction amongst U.S. forces had reached epidemic proportions. It was estimated that 10-25% (24,500 to 60,000) of lower-ranking enlisted men in South Vietnam were heroin users.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=G.I. heroin addiction epidemic in Vietnam|author=Alvin M. Shuster|date=16 May 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;15 May - September
[[Operation Phoutah]] was an RLA defensive operation against a PAVN strike from Tchepone. The RLA failed in its attempts to capture Moung Phalane.<ref name=Conboy/>{{rp|289–92}}

;16 May
The ARVN launched a new operation in the Parrot's Beak region of Cambodia killing 14 PAVN on the first day.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon reports new push in Parrot's Beak region|date=16 May 1971|page=1}}</ref>

Five U.S. soldiers from the Americal Division were killed when their armored personnel carrier hit a landmine {{convert|20|mi}} from Da Nang.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Mine kills 5 G.I.s|date=16 May 1971|page=21}}</ref>

The PAVN fired two SA-2 missiles at a USAF [[Lockheed AC-130|AC-130]] gunship operating west of the [[Ban Karai Pass]], both missed.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Missiles fired at gunship|date=17 May 1971|page=11}}</ref>

;17 May
The PAVN captured [[Paksong]], the last RLA held position on the [[Bolaven Plateau]].<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=North Vietnamese capture key Laos town near trail|author=Henry Kamm|date=18 May 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;18 May
The PAVN captured Don Hene, Laos after destroying much of the town in a rocket barrage.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Enemy overruns a 2d town in Laos|author=Henry Kamm|date=19 May 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;19 May
Former commander of the Americal Division [[Samuel W. Koster]] was demoted from [[Major general (United States)|major general]] to [[Brigadier general (United States)|brigadier general]] for his role in covering up the Mỹ Lai massacre.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=General demoted over My Lai case|author=William Beecher|date=20 May 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;20 May
A report in ''The New York Times'' stated that the U.S. was limiting the tactical and strategic capabilities of the RVNAF on the assumption that the war was winding down. The RVNAF was unable to conduct large airlifts or conduct cross-border interdiction operations necessitating a large ongoing U.S. air presence.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. limits Saigon Air Force potential|author=Craig R. Whitney|date=20 May 1971|page=1}}</ref>

In Operation Lam Son 720 the ARVN reported killing 38 PAVN at the southeastern end of the A Sầu Valley.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Allied and enemy war deaths decline|date=21 May 1971|page=6}}</ref>

;21 May
30 U.S. infantrymen, many from Company A, 1st Battalion, [[61st Infantry Regiment (United States)|61st Infantry Regiment]], were killed when a PAVN 122mm rocket hit their bunker at [[Charlie 2]].<ref>{{cite journal|title=Deadly climax of ground war on Firebase Charlie 2|journal=Veterans of Foreign Wars|author=Richard Kolb|date=May 2011}}</ref><ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|372}}

;22 May
In Paris, North Vietnamese negotiator Xuân Thủy said the issue of U.S. POWs could be resolved quickly once the U.S. set a date for full withdrawal of its forces.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Hanoi aide in interview, links pullout date and P.O.W. issue|author=Anthony Lewis|date=24 May 1971|page=2}}</ref>

;23 May
Senior U.S. military and civilian pacification advisers reported that the VC in the Mekong Delta were consolidating their position and waiting for the U.S. withdrawal.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Advisers in Mekong Delta warned on foe|author=Iver Peterson|date=24 May 1971|page=4}}</ref>

;24 May
PAVN sappers penetrated [[Cam Ranh Base]] and blew up storage tanks containing 1.5 million gallons of aviation fuel.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Enemy saboteurs invade U.S. base and blow up fuel|author=Iver Peterson|date=25 May 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;25 May
Republican Senator [[Jacob Javits]] on a visit to Saigon said that South Vietnam would need $2 billion in annual economic and military aid after a U.S. withdrawal.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Javits, in Saigon, sees need for $2 billion in aid yearly after pullout|author=Alvin Shuster|date=26 May 1971|page=11}}</ref>

;27 May
Abrams said that a "herculean effort" was required to overcome laxness amongst U.S. forces as they withdrew from South Vietnam. He was reported to have been angered by the deaths of U.S. troops at Fire Base Mary Ann and Charlie 2 which he blamed on a lack of discipline and security precautions.<ref name=NYT28May>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Abrams cautions on laxity by G.I.s|date=27 May 1971|page=13}}</ref>

In Operation Lam Son 720 the ARVN reported killing 47 PAVN just east of the A Sầu Valley for the loss of three ARVN. 26 VC and seven RF/PF were killed in a convoy ambush on Highway 1.<ref name=NYT28May/>

;28-9 May
A PAVN battalion attacked ARVN forces near Snuol losing 56 killed.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Foe repulsed in Cambodia Saigon says|date=28 May 1971|page=3}}</ref> A further PAVN attack resulted in 120 PAVN and eight ARVN killed.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Foe said to fail again at Snuol|date=29 May 1971|page=4}}</ref>

;29-30 May
Elements of the 38th Regiment and 91st Sapper Battalion attacked Dai Xuyen District, south of Da Nang, where over 80,000 South Vietnamese civilians, including high government officials, had gathered for a religious ceremony. The battle raged throughout the day and into the following night before the PAVN/VC fell back, leaving behind over 200 dead while Allied losses were five killed. 20 civilians died in the fighting and homes in the area suffered extensive damage.<ref name=USMC/>{{rp|247}}

;30 May
[[Chief of Naval Operations]] Admiral Elmo R. Zumwalt gave sailors in South Vietnam a 30-day amnesty to seek drug treatment.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Navy announces amnesty on drugs|date=30 May 1971|page=1}}</ref>

Twelve civilians were killed and 11 wounded in a PAVN/VC rocket attack on Da Nang.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Rockets kill 12 at Danang|date=30 May 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;31 May 
Kissinger in secret peace negotiations with North Vietnam in Paris introduced a new proposal for a U.S. withdrawal from South Vietnam, a ceasefire in place and an exchange of prisoners. The ceasefire in place was a key concession because it would allow PAVN soldiers to remain in South Vietnam at least temporarily.<ref name=Asselin/>{{rp|27–8}}

RF/PF forces supported by armor killed 154 PAVN/VC for the loss of four killed in fighting at [[Điện Bàn]].<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Enemy launches largest number of raids in month|date=31 May 1971|page=1}}</ref>

The PAVN ejected ARVN forces from Snuol in heavy fighting after PAVN antiaircraft fire and monsoon rains prevented resupply.<ref name=NYT1Jun>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Enemy reported to capture Snoul|date=1 June 1971|page=1}}</ref> The ARVN denied they had been routed and claimed to have killed over 700 PAVN during the withdrawal.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon denies rout by enemy at Snuol|author=Iver Peterson|date=2 June 1971|page=3}}</ref>

A bomb explosion in a government tax building in Saigon killed five civilians and wounded 12.<ref name=NYT1Jun/>

Only 13 of 570 disabled PAVN/VC prisoners accepted an offer of repatriation to North Vietnam.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=All but 13 of 570 P.O.W.s refuse to return to Hanoi|author=Alvin Shuster|date=1 June 1971|page=1}}</ref>

1,000 U.S. servicemen based in the [[United Kingdom]] held an antiwar protest outside the [[Embassy of the United States, London|U.S. Embassy]] in [[London]].<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=G.I. unit in Britain assails Vietnam War|date=1 June 1971|page=2}}</ref>

The Cambodian Government called for peace talks with North Vietnam once North Vietnamese and VC forces left Cambodia. The North Vietnamese negotiators in Paris rejected the proposal as "nonsense" and called for the restoration of Sihanouk.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Cambodians willing to discuss peace with North Vietnam|date=1 June 1971|page=7}}</ref>

U.S. Army commanders acknowledged that troops withdrawals had made some base areas more vulnerable but that it had not led to increased vulnerability for troops in the field.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Pullout not seen as threat to G.I.s in field|author=Iver Peterson|date=1 June 1971|page=6}}</ref>

==June==
;2 June
Brigadier General [[John W. Donaldson]] was charged with the murder of six Vietnamese civilians during operations in November 1968-January 1969 while flying in his helicopter over Quảng Ngãi Province. A colonel at the time of the alleged crimes, he was the first U.S. general charged with war crimes since 1902 and the highest ranking American to be accused of war crimes during the war. The charges were eventually dismissed due to lack of evidence.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://apps.dtic.mil/dtic/tr/fulltext/u2/a955421.pdf|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190711170223/https://apps.dtic.mil/dtic/tr/fulltext/u2/a955421.pdf|url-status=live|archive-date=July 11, 2019|title=1971 Command History Volume II|publisher=United States Military Assistance Command Vietnam|page=J-21|accessdate=24 July 2020}}</ref><ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|373}}

A U.S. helicopter crashed {{convert|24|mi}} northwest of Saigon killing seven Americans.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Copter crash kills 7|date=2 June 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;3 June
U.S. aircraft carried out large bombing raids against three PAVN divisions around Snuol.<ref name=NYT3Jun>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Heavy U.S. raids reported near Snoul|date=3 June 1971|page=3}}</ref>

PAVN mortars hit South Vietnamese Marines at [[Firebase Fuller]] killing six while the Marines claimed nine PAVN killed.<ref name=NYT3Jun/>

A PAVN attack on Firebase 5 southwest of [[Đắk Tô]] killed four ARVN.<ref name=NYT3Jun/>

Hanoi announced that it would not accept the return of disabled POWs even though a ship carrying the 13 PAVN/VC prisoners for the exchange had already set sail. The prisoners were returned to Da Nang.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Hanoi now says it won't accept return of P.O.W.s|date=4 June 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;4 June
ANK forces fought three PAVN/VC regiments for control of the Vihear Suor marshes {{convert|15|mi}} east of Phnom Penh with Allied airstrikes killing at least 15 PAVN/VC.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Fight erupts again in Cambodian marsh|date=5 June 1971|page=5}}</ref>

;4-5 June
[[File:Vietnam....A Marine stands watch in an observation tower as Lieutenant Commander McElroy, the 3rd Battalion, 26th... - NARA - 532465.tif|thumb|right|Hill 950 in 1967]]
The PAVN attack [[Hill 950]], a U.S. [[Military Assistance Command, Vietnam – Studies and Observations Group]] (MACV-SOG) operations base and [[United States Army Security Agency]] radio relay site overlooking the Khe Sanh plateau. Many of the personnel at the base were evacuated by helicopter, but approximately 22 remained to defend the base and destroy its secret communications system and were either captured or evaded into the surrounding area.<ref>{{cite book|last=Long|first=Lonnie|title=Unlikely Warriors: The Army Security Agency's Secret War in Vietnam 1961-1973|publisher=iUniverse|year=2013|isbn=9781475990591|page=355}}</ref>

;5 June
The CIA identified at least 21 [[opium]] refineries in the [[Golden Triangle (Southeast Asia)|Golden Triangle]] that supplied heroin to U.S. forces in South Vietnam.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=C.I.A. identifies 21 Asian Opium Refineries|date=6 June 1971|page=2}}</ref>

;6 June
In heavy fighting around Khe Sanh, South Vietnamese Marines reported killing 174 PAVN for the loss of ten Marines, with U.S. helicopter gunships accounting for 58 of the PAVN.<ref name=NYT6Jun>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Battles reported in Khe Sanh area|date=6 June 1971|page=3}}</ref> At 02:00 the PAVN attacked Marines {{convert|1.5|mi}} northwest of [[Firebase Sarge]] losing 83 killed for the loss of two Marines.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon reports 83 of foe slain; clashes continue near border|date=7 June 1971|page=4}}</ref>

A PAVN rocket attack on Da Nang killed seven civilians and three ARVN.<ref name=NYT6Jun/>

Democratic Senator [[Stuart Symington]] said that the U.S. was spending hundreds of millions of dollars on a clandestine war in Laos, while Senator Edward Kennedy accused the Nixon Administration of whitewashing U.S. involvement there.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Two Senators assail U.S. on Laos war|date=7 June 1971|page=5}}</ref>

;6-7 June
In the [[Battle of Long Khánh]] the [[3rd Battalion, Royal Australian Regiment]] attacked a PAVN/VC base camp in [[Long Khánh Province]]. The battle resulted in five VC and three Australians killed.<ref>{{cite book|last=Coulthard-Clark |first=Chris |title=The Encyclopaedia of Australia's Battles|year=2001|edition=Second|publisher=Allen & Unwin|isbn=1-86508-634-7|page=292}}</ref>

;7 June
U.S. helicopter gunships sank 11 sampans in the Mekong Delta killing 22 VC.<ref name=NYT7Jun>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Copters sink 11 sampans|date=7 June 1971|page=4}}</ref>

A PAVN rocket attack on Da Nang killed three civilians and wounded ten.<ref name=NYT7Jun/>

The Pentagon reported that the PAVN were upgrading and expanding their road network in Laos despite the onset of the monsoon which would normally halt construction and repair work.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Hanoi reported pushing supply effort, repairing Laos route west of Trail|author=William Beecher|date=8 June 1971|page=8}}</ref>

The State Department acknowledged that the U.S. was financing approximately 4,500 Thai "volunteers" in Laos, saying that it predated a 1970 congressional ban on the use of mercenaries.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. defends support of Thai troops in Laos|author=John W. Finney|date=8 June 1971|page=11}}</ref>

;7-10 June
A 2,000 strong ARVN Ranger force conducted a sweep of southeastern Cambodia killing 90 PAVN/VC for 11 Rangers killed.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. war deaths, 19 for week, at lowest level since late '65|date=11 June 1971|page=4}}</ref>

;9 June
PAVN forces attacked the U.S. trained 1st and 7th ANK Regiments at Prey Thom in the Vihear Suor marshes {{convert|12|mi}} east of Phnom Penh killing more than 80 ANK.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Enemy attacks near Phnom Penh|date=9 June 1971|page=7}}</ref> PAVN forces captured Sráng {{convert|25|mi}} southeast of Phnom Penh, inflicting heavy losses on ANK forces.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|373}}

ARVN General [[Nguyễn Văn Hiếu]] was relieved of command of the 5th Division on the recommendation of Abrams and [[Nguyễn Văn Minh]] due to his mishandling of the Battle of Snuol.<ref name=Clarke/>{{rp|476}}

The Senate approved 67:11 a draft ceiling of 130,000 for Fiscal Year 1972 and 140,000 for Fiscal Year 1973.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Senate approves ceiling on draft for next 2 years|author=David E. Rosenbaum|date=10 June 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;9-11 June
[[Operation Phiboonpol]] was an offensive by four RLA battalions to capture the Bolaven Plateau. Faced with strong opposition from the PAVN the RLA only managed to secure a tenuous position on the edge of the plateau after suffering heavy losses.<ref name=Conboy/>{{rp|286–9}}

;10 June
The South Vietnamese Ministry of Economy announced that it would open bidding for oil exploration concessions of its continental shelf within the year.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=18 U.S. concerns may seek Saigon's oil|author=Gloria Emerson|date=11 June 1971|page=4}}</ref>

;11 June
The Nixon Administration ordered that U.S. forces could no longer participate in reconnaissance missions into Laos, ending MACV-SOG's role in such operations.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Americans are barred from spy raids in Laos|author=William Beecher|date=11 June 1971|page=1}}</ref>

The Cambodian Government ordered a news blackout of the fighting in the Vihear Suor marshes with intense fighting and antiaircraft fire reported around the ANK stronghold at Kompong Ampil. Two ANK generals were reported to have flown to Saigon to seek South Vietnamese military assistance.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Cambodian battle continues|date=11 June 1971|page=7}}</ref>

;12 June
Thiệu reshuffled his cabinet ahead of the presidential election later in the year, moving or removing several key allies of his rival Kỳ, most notably Economy Minister Nguyễn Bích Hue.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon announced changes in cabinet|author=Craig R. Whitney|date=11 June 1971|page=4}}</ref>

The Cambodian Government claimed that 350 PAVN/VC had been killed in the Vihear Suor fighting.<ref name=NYT13Jun>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Enemy drive is said to peril south Laos|date=13 June 1971|page=3}}</ref>

PAVN forces supported by tanks overran Ban Nhik and moved to within {{convert|17|mi}} of the regional capital, [[Pakse]].<ref name=NYT13Jun/>

A VC ambush of a convoy on Highway 19 resulted in nine VC, three ARVN and three South Vietnamese construction workers killed.<ref name=NYT14Jun>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Fighting rages for 5 1/2 hours at besieged Vietnamese base|date=14 June 1971|page=6}}</ref>

Eleven civilians were killed and nine wounded when a bus hit a VC land mine on Highway 14.<ref name=NYT14Jun/>

;13 June
''The New York Times'' began publishing the [[Pentagon Papers]].<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|373}}

;14-15 June
In fighting near Firebase 5 ARVN Airborne forces killed 92 PAVN for the loss of one killed.<ref name=NYT15Jun>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=4 Americans reported killed by North Vietnamese in ambush|date=15 June 1971|page=2}}</ref>

;15 June
PAVN ambushed a patrol of the U.S. 3rd Brigade, 1st Cavalry Division near [[Xuân Lộc District]] killing four U.S. for the loss of nine PAVN killed.<ref name=NYT15Jun/>

The Nixon Administration obtained an interim court ruling to stop the publication of the Pentagon Papers.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Judge, at request of U.S., halts Times Vietnam series four days pending hearing on injunction|author=Fred P. Graham|date=16 June 1971|page=1}}</ref>

The Nixon Administration was reported to be conducting a review on how to counter any new PAVN offensive in 1972.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. plans ways to bolster Saigon for '72 enemy push|author=William Beecher|date=16 June 1971|page=1}}</ref>

MACV announced that U.S. battalion advisory teams to the ARVN would be phased out within the next two weeks, with advisors now at the regiment level.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Advisory team cutback|date=16 June 1971|page=15}}</ref>

ARVN forces killed 11 VC in the Rung Sat Special Zone.<ref name=NYT17Jun>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Troops sweep Saigon region, preparing for parade Saturday|date=17 June 1971|page=8}}</ref>

;16 June
Twenty PAVN attacked four [[Khmer National Navy]] guardposts in Phnom Penh, killing four sailors.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Attack in Phnom Penh reported|date=17 June 1971|page=8}}</ref>

The Senate voted 55:42 to reject the McGovern–Hatfield plan that would have withdrawn all U.S. forces by the end of 1971.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Senate, 55 to 42 defats McGovern-Hatfield plan|author=John W. Finney|date=17 June 1971|page=1}}</ref> The [[United States Conference of Mayors]] voted 54:49 to urge Nixon to withdraw all U.S. forces by the end of 1971.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Mayors demand pullout by 1972|author=John Herbers|date=17 June 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;17 June
More than 10,000 ARVN troops conducted security sweeps around Saigon ahead of an Armed Services Day parade on 19 June.<ref name=NYT17Jun/>

The U.S. and [[Japan]] signed a treaty to return [[Okinawa Prefecture|Okinawa]] to Japanese control. Under the treaty U.S. bases on Okinawa could no longer be used for military operations without Japanese government approval.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Pact for Okinawa return signed|author=Terence Smith|date=18 June 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;18 June
Approximately 400 PAVN attacked Firebase Sarge, they were forced back by the 200 Marine defenders with U.S. air support with 95 PAVN and 13 Marines killed.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon's Marines said to repel attack on outpost near DMZ|date=18 June 1971|page=2}}</ref>

;19 June
At the Armed Forces Day parade in Saigon Thiệu repeated his Four Nos. In Phú Yên Province 18 RF/PF were killed in a VC attack on their outpost, while 155 VC were claimed killed.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon parades Army's growing power|author=Iver Peterson|date=20 June 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;20-1 June
B-52s bombed PAVN fortifications, storage areas and infiltration routes on the Khe Sanh plateau and around Firebase Fuller.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=B-52s again raid infiltration area just below DMZ|author=Craig R. Whitney|date=21 June 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;22 June
Congress by a 57:42 vote adopted an amendment authored by Senator [[Mike Mansfield]] urging the withdrawal of U.S. forces from South Vietnam within nine months of the release of all U.S. POWs. The Nixon Administration said the resolution was non-binding.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Senate backs a Vietnam pullout in 9 months if P.O.W.s freed|author=John W. Finney|date=23 June 1971|page=1}}</ref> The amendment was rejected in the House 219:176 in a vote on 28 June.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|375}}

;23 June
The ANK claimed that its forces had overrun the headquarters of the PAVN 272nd Regiment, 9th Division in the Vihear Suor marshes and killed 120 PAVN.<ref name=NYT24Jun>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Cambodians report headquarters lost by enemy regiment|date=24 June 1971|page=9}}</ref>

The ARVN killed 31 PAVN at Firebase Fuller while losing eight killed. U.S. helicopter gunships killed a further seven PAVN in the area.<ref name=NYT24Jun/>

;24 June
Private Ken Harding died of wounds becoming the last of 37 New Zealand soldiers to die in the war.<ref>{{cite book|last=McGibbon|first=Ian|title=New Zealand's Vietnam War: A history of combat, commitment and controversy|publisher=Exisle, Auckland NZ & Ministry of Culture and Heritage|year=2010|isbn=978-0-908988969|pages=462–3}}</ref>

;24-8 June
The PAVN overran Firebase Fuller and were then subjected to an intense aerial bombardment before three ARVN battalions were flown in by helicopters of the U.S. 101st Airborne to recapture the area around the base.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon retakes key base at DMZ|author=Iver Peterson|date=25 June 1971|page=1}}</ref> On 28 June ARVN troops reoccupied the base but it was soon abandoned due to the damage sustained in the fighting. The South Vietnamese claimed that 496 PAVN had been killed at Fuller for the loss of 29 Marines/ARVN.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon's troops reoccupy camp|date=25 June 1971|page=12}}</ref>

;26 June
The last units of the 3rd Marine Amphibious Brigade left Da Nang.<ref name=USMC/>{{rp|246}}

;27 June 
North Vietnam negotiators [[Le Duc Tho]] and Xuân Thủy responded to Kissinger's 31 May proposal with a nine-point "bargaining proposal".  This was the first time that the North Vietnamese had indicated a willingness to negotiate rather than presenting unilateral demands.<ref name=Asselin/>{{rp|28}}

Colonel [[David Hackworth]] appeared on [[American Broadcasting Company|ABC]]'s ''[[Issues and Answers]]'' where he strongly criticized U.S. commanders in Vietnam, said the war could not be won, and called for U.S. withdrawal.<ref name=Hammond/>{{rp|498–9}}

MACV reported that at least two PAVN regiments had infiltrated across the DMZ in the preceding weeks for operations before the rainy season began in August. In response the U.S. had increased B-52 missions against infiltration routes.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Crossing of DMZ by 2 regiments of foe reported|author=Alvin Shuster|date=28 June 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;30 June
The [[United States Supreme Court]] ruled 6:3 that ''The New York Times'' could resume publication of the Pentagon Papers.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|375}}

Fighting intensified below the DMZ with the ARVN killing 92 PAVN for the loss of 15 killed near [[Firebase Gio Linh]]. ARVN troops engaged PAVN west of Firebase Fuller killing 38 PAVN for the loss of three killed. B-52s and other U.S. jets conducted airstrikes throughout the area in support of the ARVN.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Fighting intensified below DMZ; B-52's strike foe|author=Alvin Shuster|date=1 July 1971|page=33}}</ref>

[[William Colby]] resigned as head of [[Civil Operations and Revolutionary Development Support]] (CORDS) and returned to the U.S.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Colby, U.S. chief of pacification for Vietnam, gives up duties and returns home|author=Alvin Shuster|date=1 July 1971|page=34}}</ref>

==July==
Kissinger made the first of two secret visits to China that paved the way for [[Richard Nixon's 1972 visit to China]]. Following this meeting Zhou Enlai travelled to Hanoi to advise the North Vietnamese leadership of the change in Sino-American relations. The North Vietnamese were outraged by this change which they correctly perceived as an attempt by the U.S. to undermine Chinese support for North Vietnam in return for a change in U.S. policy towards [[Taiwan]].<ref>{{cite book|last=Asselin|first=Pierre|title=Vietnam's American War|publisher=Cambridge University Press|year=2018|isbn=978-1107510500|pages=185–6}}</ref>

;1 July
In Paris the VC offered to return all U.S. POWs if all U.S. forces were withdrawn by the end of 1971 and Vietnamization was halted.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Vietcong offer to trade P.O.W.s for pullout in '71|author=Henry Giniger|date=2 July 1971|page=1}}</ref>

The [[Twenty-sixth Amendment to the United States Constitution]] came into effect, lowering the voting age from 21 to 18. The amendment was driven in part by the draft which conscripted men between the ages of 18 and 21.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|375}}

;1 July to 4 October
The ARVN 23rd Division conducted an unnamed operation in Quảng Ngãi Province resulting in 324 PAVN/VC killed.

;3 July
Kissinger arrived in Saigon for meetings with senior U.S. and South Vietnamese commanders.<ref name=NYT3Jul>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=No threat is seen to Allied control south of DMZ|author=Craig R. Whitney|date=3 July 1971|page=1}}</ref>

MACV stated that the recent attacks below the DMZ were intended to demoralize South Vietnamese forces. MACV said that most of the airstrikes and helicopter support for ARVN operations were carried out by U.S. forces and that once U.S. forces left the area, airstrikes would become "problematical" and the ARVN would become more reliant on ground transport.<ref name=NYT3Jul/>

;5 July
A PAVN mortar attack on Da Nang Air Base killed three U.S. servicemen.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=3 G.I.s are reported killed by enemy shells at Danang|date=5 July 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;6-7 July
[[1971 Pacific typhoon season|Typhoon Harriet (Neneng)]] hit near the DMZ causing significant disruption to military operations on both sides. All U.S. helicopters grounded and ground movement was severely limited. Despite the intensity of the storm, damage was relatively light, with [[Camp Eagle (Vietnam)|Camp Eagle]] reporting some roofs blown off from 120&nbsp;km/h (75&nbsp;mph) winds.<ref>{{cite news|agency=Associated Press|newspaper=The Spokesman-Review|date=July 7, 1971|access-date=April 10, 2013|title=Typhoon Harriet Stalls Viet Battles|page=2|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=De9LAAAAIBAJ&sjid=_uwDAAAAIBAJ&dq=typhoon%20harriet&pg=7223%2C1983428}}</ref> In Da Nang, between {{convert|8|and|10|in|mm|abbr=on}} of rain fell and strong winds knocked out power to the area.<ref>{{cite news|agency=Associated Press|newspaper=The Fort Scott Tribune|date=July 6, 1971|access-date=April 10, 2013|title=Typhoon Curtails U.S. Operations|page=1|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=gNgfAAAAIBAJ&sjid=-dgEAAAAIBAJ&dq=typhoon%20harriet&pg=3823%2C3506953}}</ref> A 24‑hour maximum rainfall of {{convert|10.16|in|mm|abbr=on}} was measured in [[Camp Evans (Vietnam)|Camp Evans]]. Throughout Vietnam, four people were killed and fourteen others were reported missing. [[Thừa Thiên Province]] sustained the most significant damage, with 2,500&nbsp;homes damaged or destroyed.<ref>{{cite web|work=Joint Typhoon Warning Center|publisher=United States Navy|year=1972|access-date=April 10, 2013|title=Annual Tropical Cyclone Report: Typhoon Harriet|pages=131–136|url=http://www.usno.navy.mil/NOOC/nmfc-ph/RSS/jtwc/atcr/1971atcr.pdf|archive-date=September 25, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180925075943/https://www.usno.navy.mil/NOOC/nmfc-ph/RSS/jtwc/atcr/1971atcr.pdf|url-status=dead}}</ref>

;7 July
In testimony before the House Foreign Affairs subcommittee Republican Representative [[Robert H. Steele]] identified [[II Corps (South Vietnam)|II Corps]] commander Major general [[Ngô Du]] and RLA commander [[Ouane Rattikone]] as heroin traffickers.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=A Saigon General named as a trafficker in heroin|date=8 July 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;8 July
The U.S. rejected the VC 1 July peace proposal.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|376}}

;8-9 July
The U.S. 1st Brigade, 5th Infantry Division (Mechanized) turned over control of the DMZ to the ARVN 1st Division.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|376}}

;8-15 July
U.S. helicopters transported 1,500 ARVN troops into the Parrot's Beak, Cambodia, meeting minimal opposition. The operation ended on 15 July with no significant contact made.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|376}}

;11 July
U.S. Phantom jets destroyed PAVN antiaircraft guns in the Mụ Giạ Pass in the 46th "protective reaction" strike of the year after the guns fired on U.S. jets bombing the Ho Chi Minh Trail in Laos.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|376}}

;13 July
A USAF court-martial found Captain Thomas S. Culver guilty of participating in 31 May antiwar demonstration in London, in breach of military regulations. He was reprimanded and fined $1,000.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Reprimand and fine for antiwar officer|author=Bernard Weinraub|date=15 July 1971|page=1}}</ref>

RLA forces with U.S. air support recaptured the Plain of Jars meeting minimal opposition.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|376}}

;14 July
Kỳ accused Thiệu of rigging the vote so that he would win the presidential election.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Ky says Thieu tries to rig vote|author=Alvin Shuster|date=15 July 1971|page=3}}</ref>

U.S. forces began land-clearing operations in the [[Iron Triangle (Vietnam)|Iron Triangle]] to convert the PAVN/VC base area into farmland.<ref name=NYT15Jul>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Allied bulldozers are clearing and ex-Vietcong base for crops|date=15 July 1971|page=3}}</ref>

The ARVN killed 22 PAVN/VC in two actions in Quảng Ngãi Province and killed 19 PAVN/VC in the [[U Minh Thượng National Park|U Minh forest]].<ref name=NYT15Jul/>

;15 July
Nixon announced that he had accepted an invitation to visit China and would do so before May 1972.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Nixon will visit China before next May to seek a normalization of relations; Kissinger met Chou in Peking last week|author=John Herbers|date=16 July 1971|page=1}}</ref>

The House Government Operations subcommittee disclosed that 26,843 South Vietnamese had been "neutralized" over a 14-month period under the Phoenix Program with 9,820 killed, 7,751 sentenced and 9,161 rallied.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=House unit discloses civilian killings in U.S. backed program|author=Felix Belair|date=16 July 1971|page=4}}</ref>

;17 July
The Politburo of North Vietnam instructed its negotiators in Paris not to make any further concessions to the United States.<ref name=Asselin/>{{rp|29}}

The departure of U.S. forces was reported to be contributing to a decline in population security in the provinces around Saigon.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Enemy activity on rise near Saigon as militiamen replace Allied troops|author=Craig R. Whitney|date=18 July 1971|page=3}}</ref> Meanwhile, the departure of the USMC from Da Nang had led to increased unemployment in the area.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=In Danang area, unemployment is the enemy also|author=Gloria Emerson|date=18 July 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;19 July
The South Vietnamese Government proposed a ceasefire and reunification of North and South Vietnam through internationally supervised elections.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon asks Hanoi to join in truce and set election|date=19 July 1971|page=1}}</ref>

The North Vietnamese Government accused the U.S. of dividing socialist countries without specifically referring to Nixon's planned visit to China.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Hanoi accuses U.S. on Nixon doctrine|date=19 July 1971|page=8}}</ref>

Testifying before the House Foreign Operations and Government Information Subcommittee, William Colby acknowledged that the Phoenix Program had resulted in "occasional" political assassinations.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. aide defends pacification program in Vietnam despite killing of civilians|date=20 July 1971|page=2}}</ref>

;20 July
Thai forces constructed a series of bases along the border in [[Sayaboury Province]], northwestern Laos.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|377}}

;21 July
The [[Embassy of the United States, Saigon|U.S. Embassy]] in Saigon released an assessment of the security situation in South Vietnam showing that the PAVN/VC had made gains in areas evacuated by U.S. forces, particularly Quảng Trị, Quảng Nam and Bình Định provinces and the Mekong Delta.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. report tells of gains by enemy|author=Tad Szulc|date=22 July 1971|page=1}}</ref>

The Soviet Union implicitly accused the U.S. and China of anti-Soviet maneuvering by republishing a Bulgarian editorial critical of Nixon's planned China visit.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Russians endorse criticism of U.S. and China on trip|author=Bernard Gwertzman|date=22 July 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;21 July to 9 August
ARVN forces conducted a series of large sweeps of southeastern Cambodia targeting the PAVN 7th Division. A clash on 22 July killed 37 PAVN. A clash on 28 July {{convert|9|mi}} southeast of [[Neak Loeung]] killed 36 PAVN and six ARVN.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|377}}<ref name=NYT28Jul>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Enemy blows up 4 U.S. helicopters|date=28 July 1971|page=4}}</ref> A clash on 29 July in the Parrot's Beak killed 72 PAVN.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=South Vietnames report a sharp clash in Cambodia|date=30 July 1971|page=3}}</ref> On 2 August the ARVN killed 21 PAVN south of Route 1.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Clash is reported in Cambodia sweep|date=3 August 1971|page=9}}</ref> On 7 August ARVN forces killed 79 PAVN in two clashes for the loss of 14 killed.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Allies report advances in Cambodia and Laos|date=8 August 1971|page=3}}</ref> On 9 August ARVN forces killed 24 PAVN.<ref name=NYT9Aug>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. jets attack gun positions in North in "protective reaction"|date=9 August 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;22 July
A U.S. CH-47 crashed in Quảng Tín Province killing 21 ARVN Rangers.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=21 die in copter crash|date=22 July 1971|page=10}}</ref>

Xuân Thủy accused the U.S. of "perfidious maneuvers" to divide the communist bloc in a "false offensive" for peace in Vietnam. The statement was viewed by U.S. officials as showing that the North Vietnamese had been rattled by the announcement of Nixon's China visit.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Hanoi aide in Paris accuses U.S. of "Perfidious Maneuvers"|author=Clyde Farnsworth|date=23 July 1971|page=2}}</ref>

;23 July
PAVN/VC sappers attacked the command post of the ARVN [[18th Division (South Vietnam)|18th Division]] near [[Tây Ninh (city)|Tây Ninh]], killing four ARVN for the loss of two PAVN/VC. In the Mekong Delta, VC attacked an ARVN outpost killing seven RF/PF.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Foe attacks a South Vietnamese command post|date=24 July 1971|page=2}}</ref>

;25 July
The U.S. Embassy reported "serious concern" regarding the welfare of [[Amerasian#Vietnam|children fathered by U.S. servicemen]], particularly Black servicemen. The Embassy estimated that the number of children in South Vietnamese orphanages was between 350 and 400, while the South Vietnamese Ministry of Social Welfare estimated that a further 10–15,000 mixed race children were living with their families.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Embassy in Saigon calls babies of G.I.s a "serious concern"|author=Tad Szulc|date=26 July 1971|page=1}}</ref>

; 26 July
Kissinger announced that the United States was prepared to provide $7.5 billion in aid to Vietnam, of which $2.5 billion could go to North Vietnam, and to withdraw all American forces within nine months.<ref name=Asselin/>{{rp|28}}

The court-martial began of Captain [[Ernest Medina]] for his role in the Mỹ Lai massacre.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Medina's first juror is tentatively accepted as Mylai court-martial opens|author=Homer Bigart|date=27 July 1971|page=5}}</ref>

''The New York Times'' reported that U.S. analysts believed that China would have limited ability to push North Vietnam towards a peace agreement.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Some U.S. analysts believe Peking could not help settle the Vietnam War except on Hanoi's terms|author=Tad Szulc|date=27 July 1971|page=7}}</ref>

;26 July - 31 October
[[Operation Sayasila]] was an RLA operation to capture [[Salavan (city)|Salavan]] and Paksong. The RLA succeeded at heavy cost with eight battalions rendered combat ineffective.<ref name=Conboy/>{{rp|304–7}}

;28 July
A PAVN/VC sapper attack on [[Lai Khê Base Camp|Lai Khê]] destroyed four U.S. helicopters and damaged a fifth.<ref name=NYT28Jul/>

The South Vietnamese Government announced the resumption of a controversial plan to resettle [[Montagnard (Vietnam)|Montagnard]] people away from PAVN/VC-controlled areas in the Central Highlands. The U.S. opposed the plan.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Vietnamese plan to resume shifting hill tribesmen|author=Craig Whitney|date=28 July 1971|page=1}}</ref>

The Nixon Administration announced the suspension of manned and unmanned reconnaissance flights over China to avoid any incidents before Nixon's visit to China.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. spy flights over China ended to avoid incident|author=William Beecher|date=29 July 1971|page=1}}</ref>

It was announced that U.S., South Vietnamese and Thai narcotics agents had seized heroin and opium worth $4m in raids in Saigon and on the Gulf of Thailand.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Agents from 3 nations seize $4 million in narcotics in Vietnam|date=29 July 1971|page=9}}</ref>

[[List of ambassadors of the United States to South Korea|U.S. Ambassador to South Korea]] [[William J. Porter]] was named to succeed [[David K. E. Bruce]] as head of the U.S. delegation at the Paris Peace Talks after Bruce resigned due to ill-heath.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Porter is appointed chief U.S. delegate to the Paris talks|date=29 July 1971|page=12}}</ref>

;30 July
The PAVN attacked a Ranger camp {{convert|30|mi}} south of Pleiku resulting in 40 PAVN and three Rangers killed. A PAVN rocket attack on an ARVN base {{convert|9|mi}} southwest of Da Nang killed Colonel Edward J. Cavanaugh who was visiting the base.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Enemy is reported repulsed in attack in Central Highlands|date=31 July 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;31 July
The VC said they would provide a list of all U.S. POWs as soon as the U.S. set a firm date for withdrawal of all its forces.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=P.O.W. list pledged if U.S. sets deadline|date=1 August 1971|page=5}}</ref>

PAVN forces ambushed an Americal Division patrol south of Da Nang killing three U.S.. Meanwhile, U.S. helicopter gunships operating in the area killed 30 PAVN/VC.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. patrol reports 3 killed in ambush in hills at Danang|date=1 August 1971|page=6}}</ref>

==August==
;1 August
The U.S. and South Vietnam announced bounties of up to $11,000 for information leading to the capture of key VC leaders.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Rewards up to $11,00 set for captured Vietcong|author=Alvin Shuster|date=2 August 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;2 August
The U.S. announced its support for China joining the United Nations but opposed the ousting of Taiwan.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. backs U.N. seat for Peking, opposes ousting Taiwan|author=Tad Szulc|date=3 August 1971|page=1}}</ref>

The Nixon Administration acknowledged that the CIA was maintaining a 30,000 man irregular force of the RLA in Laos. The report also described Chinese road construction around Muong Sai and the buildup of antiaircraft artillery in northern Laos, making the area one of the most highly defended places in the world.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=C.I.A. says it maintains army in Laos|author=John Finney|date=3 August 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;3 August
[[International Voluntary Services]] announced that it would end its 15-year presence in South Vietnam by the end of August as its funding from the U.S. Government was withdrawn.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. voluntary unit leaving Vietnam|author=Gloria Emerson|date=4 August 1971|page=3}}</ref>

''The New York Times'' reported increased drug use among South Vietnamese youths.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=[[The New York Times]]|title=Drug use up among Vietnamese|first=Gloria|last=Emerson|authorlink=Gloria Emerson|date=4 August 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;4 August
The court-martial began of Colonel [[Oran Henderson]] for his role in the Mỹ Lai massacre.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=[[The New York Times]]|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1971/08/05/archives/courtmartial-of-mylai-colonel-recessed-after-jury-is-picked.html|title=Court-martial of Mylai Colonel recessed after jury is picked|author=Douglas Robinson|date=5 August 1971|page=6}}</ref>

;5 August - 21 September
Operation [[Phou Khao Kham]] was an RLA operation to clear PAVN/Pathet Lao forces from Routes 13 and 7 north of Vientiane and capture [[Muang Soui]]. The RLA succeeded in recapturing Muang Soui but fails to clear the approach routes.<ref name=Conboy/>{{rp|302}}

;6 August
The 4th Battalion, [[503rd Infantry Regiment (United States)|503rd Infantry Regiment]], the last remaining unit of the 173rd Airborne Brigade redeployed from South Vietnam. The 173rd Airborne Brigade was the first U.S. Army combat unit to deploy to the war.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|378}}

;7 August
ANK forces recaptured the town of Prey Kry after an 11-day battle.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|378}}

;9 August
U.S. jets attacked PAVN antiaircraft guns in North Vietnam in the 48th "protective reaction" strike of the year. ARVN forces killed 48 PAVN/VC in four separate clashes in South Vietnam, losing four killed.<ref name=NYT9Aug/>

;11 August
Marine Sergeant Jon M. Sweeney was found not guilty of desertion and aiding the enemy at his court-martial. Sweeney was captured in South Vietnam and held in the North for 18 months before being released in August 1970. While captive he had made propaganda broadcasts for the North.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Marine who was held by Hanoi found not guilty on all charges|author=Walter Rugaber|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1971/08/12/archives/marine-who-was-held-by-hanoi-found-not-guilty-of-all-charges-l-i.html|date=12 August 1971|page=7}}</ref>

;12 August
ARVN General [[Duong Van Minh]] submitted evidence to the U.S. Embassy that Thiệu was rigging the presidential election scheduled for October.<ref name=Fulghum/>{{rp|104}}

PAVN attacked [[Cam Lộ]] District headquarters killing ten ARVN while also losing ten killed.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Hanoi units attack two bases near DMZ|date=12 August 1971|page=8}}</ref>

;14 August
PAVN artillery and rocket fire hit eight ARVN bases just south of the DMZ and antiaircraft fire shot down a U.S. UH-1 {{convert|6|mi}} northwest of Cam Lộ killing seven Americans on board.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Positions just south of DMZ shelled by enemy|date=14 August 1971|page=4}}</ref>

;15 August
PAVN attacked Firebase Sarge losing 29 killed for the loss of four Marines.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Foe attacks village near DMZ after strikes on Allied positions|date=15 August 1971|page=2}}</ref> After four days of attacks on South Vietnamese bases below the DMZ, the U.S. warned the North Vietnamese that it would retaliate against PAVN positions north of the DMZ.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. warns Hanoi over DMZ action|date=16 August 1971|page=1}}</ref>

The Cambodian Government announced that it was negotiating with South Vietnam to assume control of Neak Loeung from South Vietnamese forces. The Cambodians were said to be keen to assert authority in the area amid reports of atrocities conducted by the South Vietnamese.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Cambodia is planning to replace Saigon units|date=16 August 1971|page=10}}</ref>

In order to counter inflation Nixon ordered a 90-day wage-price freeze and announced that the U.S. would end international convertibility of the U.S. dollar to gold in what became known as the [[Nixon shock]].<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Nixon orders 90 day wage-price freeze|author=James Naughten|date=16 August 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;16 August
PAVN overran Baho, a Vietnamese Marine position {{convert|4|mi}} from Firebase Sarge and {{convert|9|mi}} south of the DMZ. An estimated 200 PAVN were killed in the attack, while Marine losses were described as heavy.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Foe seizes post near DMZ inflicting heavy losses|date=17 August 1971|page=3}}</ref> In response the U.S. moved heavy artillery units to [[Mai Loc Camp]] to support South Vietnamese forces.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. artillery moves up|date=18 August 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;17 August
The U.S. Embassy informed Washington that if Thiệu persisted in his efforts to make the upcoming presidential election a charade, it might cause "growing political instability in South Vietnam."<ref name=Fulghum/>{{rp|104}}

VC sappers sank the SS ''Green Bay'' at port in Qui Nhơn, it was the largest merchant ship sunk during the war and ultimately had to be scrapped.<ref name=Mercogliano/>{{rp|56}}

The USAF acknowledged that B-52s had been carrying out raids in the southern half of the DMZ.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. acknowledges raids by B-52s in buffer zone|date=18 August 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;17-8 August
An ARVN battalion from the 2nd Regiment, 1st Division engaged a PAVN battalion {{convert|2|mi}} southwest of [[Camp Carroll]], killing 142 PAVN for the loss of 22 ARVN. Meanwhile, the PAVN continued to bombard Firebases Fuller and Sarge causing ARVN losses of five killed, while nine PAVN were killed. U.S. jets, including B-52s provided air support to the ARVN.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon reports 142 of foe killed in battle near DMZ|date=19 August 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;18 August
Australia and New Zealand announced that they would withdraw their combat forces from South Vietnam.<ref name=Melson>{{cite book|last=Melson|first=Charles|title=U.S. Marines In Vietnam: The War That Would Not End, 1971–1973|publisher=History and Museums Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps|year=1991|url=https://www.usmcu.edu/Portals/218/U_S_%20Marines%20in%20Vietnam%20The%20war%20that%20would%20not%20end%201971-1973%20PCN%2019000311200.pdf|isbn=9781482384055}}{{PD-notice}}</ref>{{rp|284}}

;19 August
The U.S. Navy acknowledged that its ships were firing into the southern half of the DMZ. The Vietnamese Marines killed 11 PAVN in an ambush near Firebase Sarge.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=7th Fleet shells positions in DMZ|date=20 August 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;20 August
Minh withdrew as a candidate for president in the upcoming presidential election in South Vietnam. Minh said "I cannot put up with a disgusting farce that strips away all the people's hope of a democratic regime."<ref name=Fulghum/>{{rp|104}}

Calley's life sentence for his role in the My Lai massacre was reduced to 20 years. Calley served three and one-half years of his sentence before being paroled.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Calley sentence is cut to 20 years from life term|author=Homer Bigart|date=21 August 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;20 August - 3 December
[[Operation Chenla II]] was a major FANK military operation. The FANK failed to dislodge the PAVN/VC from Cambodian territory and suffered heavy casualties.

;21 August
The South Vietnamese withdrew all artillery and most of the Marines from Firebase Sarge, leaving only approximately 200 Marines at the base.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=B-52s join drive to slow enemy|date=21 August 1971|page=5}}</ref>

MACV ordered U.S. observation helicopters away from the DMZ after intense PAVN antiaircraft fire shot down four helicopters.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Copter missions halted near DMZ|date=22 August 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;22 August
The [[Federal Bureau of Investigation]] foiled attacks by members of the Catholic Left on two draft offices in [[Camden, New Jersey]] and [[Buffalo, New York]], arresting 25 people.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=25 Draft office raiders held in Jersey and Buffalo|author=Paul Montgomery|date=23 August 1971|page=1}}</ref>

U.S. jets carried out the 51st "protective reaction" strike of the year against a SAM site {{convert|9|mi}} from the Laos border which had been tracking a U.S. Navy jet.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. reports 2 attacks on bases in North Vietnam|date=23 August 1971|page=2}}</ref>

;23 August
Kỳ withdrew his candidacy for president in the upcoming election. Incumbent Thiệu was the only candidate remaining in the election.<ref name=Fulghum/>{{rp|104}}

ANK forces killed 60 PAVN on Route 6 {{convert|60|mi}} from Phnom Penh.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Cambodian advance reported|date=24 August 1971|page=5}}</ref>

;24 August
ARVN infantry found 76 PAVN dead during a bomb damage assessment after a B-52 strike {{convert|15|mi}} south of the DMZ. An ARVN armored unit engaged a PAVN force {{convert|1|mi}} outside a firebase near the DMZ, killing 37 for the loss of four killed.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=76 enemy dead reported found after B-52 near the DMZ|date=24 August 1971|page=5}}</ref>

U.S. forces were placed on alert ahead of possible attacks before the South Vietnamese ahead of lower house elections on 29 August.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Troops in Vietnam are put on alert by U.S. command|date=25 August 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;25 August
A PAVN/VC sapper attack on the Cam Ranh Base tri-service ammunition storage area destroyed over 6000 tons of munitions with a value of more than US$10 million.<ref>{{cite book|last=Fox|first=Roger|title=Air Base Defense in the Republic of Vietnam 1961–1973|publisher=Office of Air Force History|year=1979|url=http://www.afhso.af.mil/shared/media/document/AFD-100921-023.pdf|isbn=9781410222565|page=164|access-date=2020-07-14|archive-date=2016-11-23|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161123144536/http://www.afhso.af.mil/shared/media/document/AFD-100921-023.pdf|url-status=dead}}</ref> This was part of a surge of attacks across South Vietnam ahead of the South Vietnamese elections.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|379}}

The 173rd Airborne Brigade redeployed from South Vietnam, it was the longest continuously serving allied military unit of the war.<ref name=Melson/>{{rp|284}}

;26 August
ARVN forces killed 46 PAVN/VC in a sweep {{convert|35|mi}} south of Da Nang. Five civilians were killed and 11 homes destroyed when PAVN/VC shelled Tanlap on the southern outskirts of Da Nang.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon offensive reported|date=26 August 1971|page=12}}</ref>

;27 August
The 1st Brigade, 5th Infantry Division (Mechanized) redeployed from South Vietnam.<ref name=Melson/>{{rp|284}}

Five Americal Division soldiers were killed during an attack on their night defensive position {{convert|16|mi}} south-southwest of Da Nang.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=5 G.I.s are killed in enemy attack|date=27 August 1971|page=4}}</ref>

In fighting on the DMZ 15 PAVN and two ARVN were killed.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=New fighting along DMZ|date=27 August 1971|page=4}}</ref>

;29 August
The South Vietnamese lower house elections took place with Thiệu retaining a majority, despite gains by opposition groups particularly in Da Nang.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|379}}

;30 August
''The New York Times'' reported that despite anti-narcotics programs, heroin remained widely available to U.S. servicemen.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Drive fails to halt drug sales in Vietnam|author=Henry Kamm|date=30 August 1971|page=1}}</ref>

MACV and South Vietnamese command reported that the PAVN/VC had conducted 96 artillery attacks on the 29th and 30th in attempts to disrupt elections, killing 41 South Vietnamese soldiers and eight civilians, while 347 PAVN/VC were killed.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=3d day of attacks by foe reported|author=Henry Kamm|date=31 August 1971|page=2}}</ref>

;31 August
PAVN/VC attacks tapered off but three soldiers of the Americal Division were killed in two ambushes south of Da Nang. On [[Phú Quốc]] eight ARVN MPs and nine ARVN/VC POWs were killed in an apparent attempt to free prisoners from the [[Phú Quốc Prison]].<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Ground fighting in Vietnam drops|date=1 September 1971|page=6}}</ref>

==September==
;3 September
ANK forces claimed to have killed more than 100 soldiers of the PAVN 205th Regiment {{convert|65|mi}} northeast of Phnom Penh.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Clash in Cambodia|date=4 September 1971|page=2}}</ref>

;4 September
''The New York Times'' reported on the worsening morale and discipline within the U.S. Army.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Army is shaken by crisis in morale and discipline|author=Drummond Ayres|date=5 September 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;5 September 1970 - 8 October 1971
[[Operation Jefferson Glenn]] was the last major ground operation in which U.S. troops participated in the Vietnam War. Three battalions of the 101st Airborne Division patrolled the area west of the city of [[Huế]], called the "rocket belt", to try to prevent PAVN/VC rocket attacks. The Americans were gradually replaced by ARVN forces. The Americans and South Vietnamese claimed to have inflicted 2,026 casualties on the PAVN/VC.<ref>{{cite book|last=Tucker|first=Spencer|title=Encyclopedia of the Vietnam War: A Political, Social and Military History|publisher=ABC-CLIO|year=2000|isbn=978-1-57607-040-6|page=194}}</ref>

;6 September
''The New York Times'' reported that South Vietnamese forces were resorting to murder, looting and highway robbery in response to low pay and inactivity.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Crime said to rise in Vietnam's army|author=Iver Peterson|date=7 September 1971|page=6}}</ref>

;6-25 September
Operation Lam Son 810 was conducted by the ARVN 1st Division in northwestern Quảng Trị Province, with U.S. air support. The operation resulted in 175 PAVN and 75 ARVN killed.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|379}}

;7 September
Saigon offered food and $50,000 to Hanoi in response to severe flooding in the Hanoi area.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon offers aid in Hanoi flooding|date=7 September 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;10 September
24 ANK troops were killed in two separate PAVN attacks northwest of Phnom Penh.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=24 Cambodians killed|date=11 September 1971|page=8}}</ref>

;13 September
[[Lin Biao]], China's Minister of National Defense and [[Mao Zedong]]'s designated successor was killed with his family in an aircraft crash after a [[Project 571|botched coup]] against Mao.<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Uhalley Jr.|first1=Stephen|last2=Qiu|first2=Jin|title=The Lin Biao Incident: More Than Twenty Years Later|journal=Pacific Affairs|date=Autumn 1993|volume=66|issue=3|pages=386–398 |doi=10.2307/2759617|jstor=2759617}}</ref>

;14 September
A U.S. serviceman shot and killed a 16 year old South Vietnamese who tried to steal his watch and wounded a 14 year old in Huế, sparking anti-U.S. demonstrations in the city.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Protests erupt in Hue after G.I. kills youth|date=14 September 1971|page=15}}</ref>

Senator [[George McGovern]] on a visit to Saigon was trapped by rioters in a Saigon church and had to be rescued by South Vietnamese police and U.S. Military Police.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=McGovern rescued after Saigon riot|date=15 September 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;14-21 September
The ARVN conducted an operation against PAVN/VC bases in the U Minh Forest. The operation resulted in more than 400 PAVN/VC and 113 ARVN killed. Eleven U.S. helicopters supporting the operation were shot down, with four shot down in the initial assault.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|380}}

;15 September
A bomb exploded in the Tu Do Nightclub in Saigon killing 14 Vietnamese and one American and wounding 57 others.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon nightclub blast kills 15 and injures 57|date=16 September 1971|page=4}}</ref>

A South Vietnamese student group called the Movement Struggling for the People's Right to Life claimed to have burnt 32 U.S. vehicles since 23 August in an effort to force all Americans to leave South Vietnam immediately.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Vietnam student group attacking G.I.s and trucks|author=Gloria Emerson|date=16 September 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;15-6 September
RLA forces recaptured Paksong on the Bolaven Plateau. PAVN/Pathet Lao losses were 279 killed while RLA losses were 302 killed and 195 missing.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|380}}

;16 September
Thiệu stated that he saw no real chance for peace until after a major battle with the North, which he predicted would take place in 1973.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Thieu sees no real peace till after major '73 battle|author=Alvin Shuster|date=17 September 1971|page=2}}</ref>

PAVN/VC ambushed a 75-man ARVN patrol on Route 14 in the [[Michelin Rubber Plantation]] killing 15 ARVN and three U.S. advisers for the loss of eight PAVN/VC killed.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Vietcong ambush Government unit|date=19 September 1971|page=5}}</ref>

;20 September
PAVN artillery and rockets hit oil storage tanks in Phnom Penh starting large fires that destroyed 40% of Cambodia's civilian fuel supplies.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Large oil supplies destroyed by foe in Phnompenh raid|date=20 September 1971|page=1}}</ref>

''The New York Times'' reported on the expanded U.S. military support presence in Cambodia with a 50-man Military Equipment Delivery Team commanded by Brigadier general Theodore C. Mataxis. Diplomats within the U.S. Embassy were said to be unsuccessful in their attempts to limit the military's influence.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Military gains ground in U.S. Embassy in Cambodia|author=Craig Whitney|date=20 September 1971|page=3}}</ref>

Approximately 600 PAVN attacked [[Tây Ninh Combat Base]] with 52 PAVN killed and seven captured, ARVN losses were 21 killed.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Enemy commandos kill 21 South Vietnamese|date=21 September 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;21 September
In the [[Battle of Nui Le]] the [[4th Battalion, Royal Australian Regiment|4RAR/NZ (ANZAC) Battalion]] engaged PAVN/VC forces at Núi Lé, [[Chau Duc District]]. The battle resulted in 14 PAVN/VC and five Australians killed.

U.S. jets flew more than 200 sorties over eight hours attacking PAVN positions up to {{convert|35|mi}} north of the DMZ in response to the increased threat from PAVN artillery fire into South Vietnam and antiaircraft and SAM fire against U.S. aircraft.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. jets attack in North Vietnam|author=Iver Peterson|date=22 September 1971|page=1}}</ref>

The U.S. Senate voted 55:30 to approve the Selective Service Bill, allowing the Nixon Administration to continue the draft until June 1973.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|380}}

;22 September
Captain Ernest Medina was acquitted of all charges relating to the Mỹ Lai massacre.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|380}}

;23 September
The Cambodian Government reported that 11 soldiers and 19 civilians had been killed in PAVN attacks on two villages near Prasat, {{convert|60|mi}} southeast of Phnom Penh, 29 PAVN were reported killed.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. planes and ships attack southern half of buffer zone|date=23 September 1971|page=12}}</ref>

The North Vietnamese delegation refused to attend the Paris Peace Talks in response to the U.S. bombing raids of 21 September.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|380}}

U.S. officials warned Saigon's generals that any coup d'état against Thiệu due to the crisis over the coming presidential election would lead to an immediate end to U.S. aid.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. aides caution Saigon Generals|author=Alvin Shuster|date=23 September 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;25 September
U.S. deputy assistant for National Security Affairs Brigadier general [[Alexander Haig]] met with Thiệu and Kỳ, reportedly to try to end the crisis surrounding the presidential election and address growing anti-Americanism.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Nixon security aide holds talks with Thieu and Ky|author=Craig Whitney|date=25 September 1971|page=6}}</ref>

;26 September
[[Marine Security Guard]] Sergeant Charles "Wayne" Turberville was one of two Americans killed in a Khmer Rouge grenade attack in Phnom Penh.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://blogs.usembassy.gov/todd/honoring-the-past/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150707231204/http://blogs.usembassy.gov/todd/honoring-the-past/|url-status=dead|archive-date=7 July 2015|title=Honoring the Past|publisher=usembassy.gov|date=10 April 2013|accessdate=19 July 2020}}</ref><ref name=Melson/>{{rp|10}}

;26-9 September
PAVN forces conducted rocket and sapper attacks against South Vietnamese and ANK forces near Krek and ten South Vietnamese bases and the U.S. [[Firebase Pace]] all near the Cambodian border. 230 PAVN and 27 ARVN were killed in the attacks.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Foe hits 11 Allied bases along Cambodian border|date=27 September 1971|page=1}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=South Vietnamese border posts reported under stiff pressure|date=29 September 1971|page=16}}</ref>

;28 September
Nixon attended the second annual meeting of the [[National League of POW/MIA Families|National League of Families of American Prisoners and Missing in Southeast Asia]] and assured them that the U.S. would "eventually succeed" in securing their release.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=President at capital meeting reassures families of P.O.W.'s|date=29 September 1971|page=16}}</ref>

China and North Vietnam signed a new aid treaty providing for increased economic assistance to the North.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|381}}

;29 September
The ARVN began an operation to reopen Route 22 between Tây Ninh and Krek. U.S. forces took over ARVN positions near the border to allow ARVN units to advance into Cambodia.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|381}}

;30 September
The U.S. Senate voted 57:38 to approve an amendment proposed by Mike Mansfield calling for a full withdrawal within six months of the release of all U.S. POWs.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Pullout deadline voted by Senate|date=1 October 1971|page=5}}</ref>

;September - 22 June 1972
[[Operation Sourisak Montry VIII]] was a Thai offensive against Pathet Lao forces along the Mekong River near Xieng Lom, Laos. The operation was indecisive with the Pathet Lao retaining control of the area.<ref name=Conboy/>{{rp|315–20}}

==October==
;1 October
Kỳ attending an anti-Thiệu rally called for a boycott of the vote.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Ky, addressing anti-Thieu rally, urges vote boycott|author=Iver Peterson|date=1 October 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;2 October
ARVN forces relieved Firebase Tran Hung Dao which had been under siege by the PAVN for five days, while the PAVN continued to besiege Firebase Alpha {{convert|5|mi}} east of Krek.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Enemy again attacks base in Cambodia|date=2 October 1971|page=3}}</ref>

In fighting near Kien Thien in the Mekong Delta, 18 ARVN and 16 PAVN were killed.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|381}}

;3 October
The [[1971 South Vietnamese presidential election]] was held. Incumbent President Thiệu garnered 94.3 percent of the vote. All of Thiệu's opponents had dropped out of the race.<ref name=Fulghum/>{{rp|107}}

PAVN rockets fired into Saigon killed three civilians and wounded five. 34 other standoff attacks took place across South Vietnam ahead of the presidential election.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Enemy steps up shelling attacks|date=3 October 1971|page=4}}</ref>

[[List of heads of state of the Soviet Union|Soviet head of state]] [[Nikolai Podgorny]] on a visit to Hanoi pledged ongoing Soviet support for the North's war effort.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Podgorny greeted by Hanoi turnout; press hails Soviet|date=4 October 1971|page=4}}</ref>

;6 October
The ARVN claimed that the PAVN were retreating in southwestern Cambodia with the PAVN 141st and 209th Regiments, 7th Division and the 174th Regiment, 5th Division having lost over 450 soldiers in the recent fighting.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon's forces said to rout foe|date=6 October 1971|page=15}}</ref>

;7 October
At the UN, Cambodian Foreign Minister Koun Wick called for the area around Angkor Wat to be declared a demilitarized zone, to prevent further destruction to the temples there.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Cambodian urges DMZ for Angkor|author=Tad Szulc|date=8 October 1971|page=5}}</ref>

;8-9 October
ARVN reported killing 50 PAVN in fighting between [[Ponhea Kraek District|Krek]] and Firebase Alpha.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Clash in Cambodia reported by Saigon|date=8 October 1971|page=4}}</ref> An ARVN force of 1,200 Rangers supported by armor relieved Firebase Alpha on the 9th killing 97 PAVN for the loss of one killed.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon reports a breakthrough to battered base in Cambodia|date=10 October 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;8-11 October
The VC released U.S. Sergeant John C. Sexton Jr. in Cambodia, he had been captured on 12 August 1969. In exchange the South Vietnamese released a PAVN lieutenant. Sexton was the 31st POW released by the PAVN/VC.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|382}}

;9 October
Five U.S. soldiers at Firebase Pace near the Cambodian border refused to undertake a patrol outside the perimeter of the firebase. The combat refusal was widely reported by the media as was a letter signed by 65 American soldiers at Firebase Pace to Senator Edward Kennedy protesting that they were being ordered to participate in offensive combat operations despite U.S. policy to the contrary.<ref name=Bowman/>{{rp|291}}

;10 October
ARVN forces killed 46 PAVN for the loss of seven ARVN in fighting on Route 1 {{convert|7|mi}} northeast of Kompong Trabek, Cambodia.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Fighting reported heavy in Cambodia|date=11 October 1971|page=8}}</ref>

;12 October
''The New York Times'' reported that the Joint Chiefs had provided Laird with a draft pacification plan for Cambodia which would see military aid there increase to $500m per year by 1977.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Joint Chiefs said to devise costly Cambodia war plan|date=13 October 1971|page=1}}</ref>

South Vietnamese war veterans captured an Americal Division convoy on Route 2, {{convert|9|mi}} south of Da Nang after one of the U.S. vehicles crashed into a funeral procession vehicle. The convoy with 14 U.S. soldiers onboard was held for eight hours until a ransom of $720 and foodstuffs was paid to the veterans in "an expression of sorrow".<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Ransom frees U.S. convoy from Vietnamese crowd|date=13 October 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;13 October
PAVN/VC sappers destroyed two U.S. helicopters at [[Dĩ An Base Camp]]. A U.S. AH-1 Cobra gunship was shot down near Firebase Pace.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Enemy demolition squad destroys two U.S. copters|date=14 October 1971|page=4}}</ref>

;14 October
An ARVN armored task force clashed with PAVN forces near Krek killing 52 PAVN for the loss of five killed. Meanwhile, the PAVN/VC bombardment of Firebase Pace continued for its 19th consecutive day.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Sharp battle near Krek|date=15 October 1971|page=4}}</ref>

;15 October
Governor [[Ronald Reagan]] on a stopover in Saigon met with Thiệu and praised his reelection as a "referendum" saying "I am unable to understand why so many people in our country, especially the communications media, are so charged up by this one-man election."<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Reagan meets Thieu in Saigon and defends one-man race|author=Fox Butterfield|date=16 October 1971|page=5}}</ref>

;16 October
Lon Nol suspended the Cambodian national assembly and announced that he would rule by executive decree.<ref name=Bowman/>{{rp|291}}

In his memoirs published in ''The New York Times'', former President [[Lyndon Johnson]] described U.S. support for the [[1963 South Vietnamese coup]] that overthrew [[Ngo Dinh Diem]] as a "serious blunder" that created political chaos in South Vietnam which became the principal factor in Johnson committing U.S. ground forces to the war.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Memoir terms Kennedy role in Diem coup a blunder|author=Neil Sheehan|date=17 October 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;19 October
In fighting near Thien Ngon on the Cambodian border, ARVN Airborne forces killed 29 PAVN for the loss of seven killed. Meanwhile, in two actions near Xuân Lộc VC killed 12 ARVN for the loss of 11 killed.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Sharp fighting breaks out near Cambodia line|date=19 October 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;20 October
A USAF [[Cessna A-37 Dragonfly|A-37]] jet accidentally bombed ARVN forces near Thien Ngon killing 18 ARVN. In fighting in the U Minh Forest the ARVN killed 60 PAVN/VC for the loss of five killed.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. bombs kill 18 in Saigon unit|date=20 October 1971|page=16}}</ref>

Lon Nol declared a state of emergency in Cambodia and said that he would run the country by "ordinance". He said that he would no longer "play the game of democracy and freedom" as this was hindering the Cambodian government's fight against the communist forces.<ref name=Bowman/>{{rp|291}}

;22 October
U.S. forces withdrew from Firebase Pace, leaving the base and its four guns to an ARVN Airborne battalion which took over the base. In four separate actions around Pace the ARVN reported killing 47 PAVN.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. troops leave Cambodian border|author=Fox Butterfield|date=23 October 1971|page=2}}</ref>

;23 October
In two actions along the Cambodian border ARVN Rangers killed 23 PAVN. ARVN troops found the bodies of 53 PAVN killed by airstrikes {{convert|3|mi}} south of Firebase Pace.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Typhoon limits Vietnam action|date=24 October 1971|page=9}}</ref>

[[File:Typhoon Hester Chu Lai Helicopter Damage.jpg|thumb|Damaged hangar of the 174th Assault Helicopter Company at Chu Lai]]
;23-5 October
[[Typhoon Hester (1971)|Typhoon Hester]] made landfall over South Vietnam causing severe damage. At [[Chu Lai Base Area]], Hester damaged or destroyed 75&nbsp;percent of the structures in the base. Sustained winds and gusts in the base were estimated to have reached 130&nbsp;km/h (80&nbsp;mph) and 160&nbsp;km/h (105&nbsp;mph) respectively.<ref name=JTWCHester>{{cite web|work=Joint Typhoon Warning Center|publisher=United States Navy|year=1972|accessdate=April 8, 2013|title=Annual Tropical Cyclone Report: Typhoon Hester|url=http://www.usno.navy.mil/NOOC/nmfc-ph/RSS/jtwc/atcr/1971atcr.pdf|pages=237–240|archive-date=September 25, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180925075943/https://www.usno.navy.mil/NOOC/nmfc-ph/RSS/jtwc/atcr/1971atcr.pdf|url-status=dead}}</ref> Four hangars collapsed in the [[Chu Lai Air Base]], with total aircraft losses amounting to 36 destroyed and 87 damaged.<ref name=JTWCHester/> The 91st Evacuation Hospital was mostly destroyed and was forced to transfer patients to Qui Nhơn.<ref>{{cite news|author=United Press International|newspaper=The Bulletin|date=23 October 1971|accessdate=16 November 2020|title=Typhoon Hester batters coast of S. Vietnam|page=1|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=onYzAAAAIBAJ&sjid=t_cDAAAAIBAJ&dq=hester&pg=1831%2C5643266|location=Saigon, Vietnam}}</ref> Nearly 50&nbsp;percent of the structures at the [[Marble Mountain Air Facility]] were damaged by the storm's high winds. Heavy rains accompanying the storm caused considerable flooding in the country, approximately 370&nbsp;km (230&nbsp;mi) of coastline between Quảng Trị and Da Nang were inundated. About 90&nbsp;percent of homes in Da Nang were damaged.<ref name=JTWCHester/> Twenty-two people were killed when an RVNAF [[Douglas C-47 Skytrain|C-47]] transport crashed {{convert|5|mi}} west Qui Nhơn.<ref>{{cite news|agency=Associated Press|newspaper=Spokane Daily Chronicle|date=25 October 1971|accessdate=16 November 2020|title=Viet Storm Aid Rushed: Toll Up to 103|page=29|location=Saigon, Vietnam|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=55JYAAAAIBAJ&sjid=VPgDAAAAIBAJ&dq=storm%20hester&pg=6940%2C2461135}}</ref> Thee Americans were killed due to flying debris during the storm and twenty-one others were injured.<ref name=JTWCHester/> On 25 October, thunderstorms associated with Hester were blamed for a [[Boeing CH-47 Chinook|CH-47]] crash near [[Nha Trang]] that killed 10 Americans.<ref>{{cite news|agency=Associated Press|newspaper=Youngstown Vindicator|date=29 October 1971|accessdate=16 November 2020|title=10 Americans Die In Copter Crash|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=_kJJAAAAIBAJ&sjid=84MMAAAAIBAJ&dq=hester&pg=6213%2C7126930|location=Saigon, Vietnam|page=1}}</ref>

;24 October
In his memoirs Johnson stated that he regretted not saying more about Vietnam in his 17 January 1968 [[State of the Union]] address, saying "I did not go into details concerning the build-up of enemy forces or warn of the early major combat I believed was in the offing...This was one of those delicate situations in which we had to try to inform our own people without alerting the enemy to our knowledge of its plans. In retrospect, I think I was too cautious. If I had forecast the possibilities, the American people would have been better prepared for what was to come."<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=By Lyndon B. Johnson: The Tet Offensive|date=24 October 1971|page=42}}</ref>

;26 October
The [[United Nations General Assembly]] voted 76:35 to seat China and expel Taiwan in a diplomatic defeat for the U.S.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.N. seats China and expels Taipei|date=26 October 1971|page=1}}</ref>

In two actions in the Mekong Delta ARVN forces killed 23 PAVN/VC.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Retreat reported by Cambodian unit|date=26 October 1971|page=2}}</ref>

PAVN/VC ambushed a convoy {{convert|50|mi}} northeast of Saigon killing 18 ARVN and one American for the loss of one killed.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. truck convoy attacked in Vietnam|date=27 October 1971|page=4}}</ref>

300 antiwar protestors were arrested after sitting down in [[Pennsylvania Avenue]], Washington D.C. in the evening rush-hour.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=300 arrested in antiwar protest near White House|date=27 October 1971|page=10}}</ref>

;27-9 October
PAVN frogmen destroyed a bridge used to supply ANK forces northeast of Phnom Penh and besiege the ANK garrisons at Kompong Thom and Rumlong killing approximately 400 ANK soldiers.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|382}}

;28 October
The U.S. Senate voted 47:44 to reject the Cooper-Church amendment that would have restricted the administration to only using funds to withdraw U.S. forces from Indochina.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Senate, 47 to 44, kills fund curb on Vietnam War|author=John Finney|date=29 October 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;31 October
Thiệu was inaugurated for a new term as president, to mark the occasion the South Vietnamese amnestied 2,938 low-ranking VC POWs.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|382}}

A VC battalion attacked an ARVN position {{convert|40|mi}} northwest of Saigon killing ten ARVN for the loss of 31 VC.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon reports 31 of foe killed; Americal Division deactivated|date=1 November 1971|page=4}}</ref>

==November==
;1-9 November
[[Operation Bedrock (Laos)|Operation Bedrock]] was an RLA offensive against the PAVN 46th Battalion near Salavan. The operation succeeded in securing the rice growing area near Salavan.<ref name=Conboy/>{{rp|304–8}}

;2 November  
A U.S. Senate sub-committee issued a 300-page report "corruption, criminality, and moral compromise" at U.S. [[Post Exchange]]s in South Vietnam.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Senate PX study finds wide guilt|author=Walter Rugaber|date=3 November 1971|page=1}}</ref>

ANK forces relieved Prakham which had been under siege by the PAVN for a week, killing 291 PAVN.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|383}}

A U.S. Navy jet attacked a PAVN antiaircraft site in North Vietnam in the 70th "protective reaction" strike of the year. U.S. helicopter gunships killed 24 PAVN/VC in the Mekong Delta.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. Navy jet reported to strike missile site in North Vietnam|date=3 November 1971|page=8}}</ref>

;4 November
MACV announced that two Americans had been killed in action in the previous week, the lowest death toll since U.S. combat forces were first deployed in 1965.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. puts week's war toll at 2, concedes there might be more|author=Iver Peterson|date=5 November 1971|page=2}}</ref>

;5 November
VC ambushed an RF/PF company on the [[Cà Mau peninsula]] killing 17.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=17 South Vietnamese are killed in an ambush in Mekong Delta|date=6 November 1971|page=8}}</ref>

The U.S. State Department said that the Senate's proposed cuts to U.S. aid would cripple the South Vietnamese economy and undermine Cambodia's ability to defend itself.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=State Department says new Senate aid proposals would cripple South Vietnam|date=6 November 1971|page=9}}</ref>

;6 November
Laird concluded a three-day visit to South Vietnam and indicated that the U.S. was ready to accelerate troop withdrawals.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Laird concludes visit to Vietnam|author=Alvin Shuster|date=7 November 1971|page=11}}</ref> ''The New York Times'' reported that both the U.S. military and civil advisory effort in South Vietnam was winding down.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. cuts civilian role, too, in Vietnam|author=Iver Peterson|date=7 November 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;7 November
A report by [[Cornell University]] showed that while the Nixon Administration had reduced ground combat in South Vietnam it had maintained the use of air power in South Vietnam and increased it in Cambodia and Laos.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Study show U.S. presses air war|author=Neil Sheehan|date=8 November 1971|page=6}}</ref>

;7-8 November
PAVN forces killed 10 ANK soldiers at Bamnal, {{convert|70|mi}} northwest of Phnom Penh.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|383}}

;9 November
U.S. jets attacked [[Quang Lang Air Base]] in their deepest strike in North Vietnam of the year and also attacked antiaircraft positions around Vinh in the 73rd "protective reaction" strike of the year.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. planes raid the North again|date=9 November 1971|page=9}}</ref>

;10 November
Professor Nguyen Van Bong, leader of the Progressive Nationalist Movement and an aide were killed in a grenade attack on their car in Saigon.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon political figure is slain|date=10 November 1971|page=7}}</ref>

The PAVN conducted a rocket and sapper attack on Pochentong Airport in Phnom Penh killing 19 people and destroying two Khmer Air Force helicopters. A communications facility nearby was also hit and put out of service for several hours.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|383}}

A U.S. helicopter crashed {{convert|17|mi}} east of Saigon killing five Americans and one South Vietnamese.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=6 killed in copter crash|date=10 November 1971|page=10}}</ref>

;11 November
[[File:Americal Division casing colors, November 1971.jpg|thumb|right|MG Frederick J. Kroesen, Jr., holds the 23rd Infantry Division (Americal) colors as they are cased, [[Chu Lai Base Area]], 11 November 1971]]
The Americal Division was inactivated in a ceremony at Chu Lai Base Area. The Division's 196th Light Infantry Brigade was reconstituted as a separate brigade and remained in South Vietnam.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=The Americal Division ends role in Vietnam|date=12 November 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;12 November
Nixon announced that a further 45,000 U.S. troops would be withdrawn from South Vietnam by 1 February 1972 and that "American troops are now in a defensive position...the offensive activities of search and destroy are now being undertaken by the South Vietnamese"<ref name=Fulghum/>{{rp|25}}<ref name=Melson/>{{rp|284}}

;13 November
MACV reported that North Vietnam was massing supplies near the Ban Karai and Mụ Giạ Passes in preparation for a major offensive in 1972. Nixon warned the North that increased infiltration would lead to increased U.S. bombing.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|383}}

ANK forces abandoned Rumlong {{convert|52|mi}} northeast of Phnom Penh after a 19-day siege by the PAVN with only 30 of the 1,000 man garrison making it back to ANK lines.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|383–4}}

The ARVN repelled a PAVN attack of a firebase near Pleiku killing 163 PAVN for the loss of 29 ARVN.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|383}}

;15 November
Thiệu outlined an economic reform program that included devaluation of the [[South Vietnamese đồng]].<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Thieu, in address to nation, orders economic reform|author=Iver Peterson|date=15 November 1971|page=1}}</ref> Saigon food prices rose 50% as a result of the devaluation of the đồng.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon's food prices rise 50% after devaluation|author=Iver Peterson|date=17 November 1971|page=5}}</ref>

Ernest Medina, testifying at the trial of Oran Henderson, said that he had lied to Henderson about the civilian death toll at Mỹ Lai and had "not been completely candid" in sworn statements to Army investigators.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Medina says he lied to Colonel about Mylai toll|author=Douglas Robinson|date=16 November 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;16 November
Two U.S. helicopters collided {{convert|70|mi}} northwest of Saigon killing all four crewmen, while another helicopter crashed {{convert|21|mi}} west of Saigon killing three ARVN.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=2 copters collide, killing 4 Americans|date=16 November 1971|page=12}}</ref>

''The New York Times'' reported on the poor conditions in South Vietnam's largest military hospital at Cong Hoa in Saigon with 1,800 beds for nearly 4,000 patients and weekly average casualties in October of 764.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=South-Vietnamese wounded jam military hospitals|author=Gloria Emerson|date=17 November 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;16-8 November
U.S. helicopter gunships flew attack missions in support of ANK forces trying to regain Tuol Leap {{convert|10|mi}} southwest of Phnom Penh which had been captured by the PAVN and USAF airstrikes increased across the country in support of the ANK.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|384}}

;17 November 
[[Prime Minister of Thailand]] [[Thanom Kittikachorn]] seized complete power and imposed martial law. He pledged ongoing support for U.S. policy in Indochina.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|384}}

;19 November
The [[List of Catholic bishops in the United States|American Bishops of the Roman Catholic Church]] passed a resolution calling for an end to the war "with no further delay".<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. Catholic Bishops call for end to Indochina War|author=Eleanor Blau|date=20 November 1971|page=1}}</ref>

Cambodian officials flew to Saigon to request the dispatch of ARVN engineers and artillery forces to relieve a 20,000 man ANK task force that had been cut off on Route 6 northeast of Phnom Penh.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon says Cambodians seek artillery|date=20 November 1971|page=5}}</ref>

;20 November
A VPAF MiG fired a missile at a cell of B-52s attacking the Ban Karai Pass.<ref name=Hartsook>{{cite web|url=https://media.defense.gov/2011/Mar/23/2001330094/-1/-1/0/AFD-110323-039.pdf|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201011212638/https://media.defense.gov/2011/Mar/23/2001330094/-1/-1/0/AFD-110323-039.pdf|url-status=dead|archive-date=October 11, 2020|title=The Air Force in Southeast Asia Shield for Vietnamization and Withdrawal 1971|publisher=Office of Air Force History|author=E Hartsook|date=July 1976|access-date=4 October 2020}}{{PD-notice}}</ref>{{rp|30}}

;21 November
[[Operation Thao La]] was an RLA dry season offensive to capture the Bolaven Plateau. The RLA secured Tha Theng and Ban Phong but lost Salavan and Paksong. The operation resulted in 1,204 PAVN and 399 RLA killed.<ref name=Conboy/>{{rp|308–9}}

;22 November
U.S. Navy jets attacked antiaircraft positions near Vinh in the 80th "protective reaction" strike of the year.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Bombing raid reported|date=22 November 1971|page=4}}</ref>

;22 November to 30 December
Approximately 25,000 ARVN and 2,500 ANK conduct operations against PAVN forces near Chup initially meeting minimal resistance, while killing 36 PAVN on the first day. The PAVN 5th and 7th Divisions withdrew further into Cambodia to avoid the slow ARVN attack. The operation resulted in 1,336 PAVN and 86 ARVN killed, most of the PAVN casualties resulted from airstrikes.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|384}}<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=New drive reported opened in Central Highlands|author=Iver Peterson|date=1 December 1971|page=18}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon ends Cambodia drive|date=31 December 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;23 November
As U.S. forces continued to leave South Vietnam it was reported that a major Northern offensive was expected in early 1972 as the PAVN built up its forces north of the DMZ.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=G.I.'s leave as Saigon's problems grow|author=Alvin Shuster|date=26 November 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;28 November
A US Army CH-47 carrying five crew and 28 soldiers from the [[327th Infantry Regiment (United States)|327th Infantry Regiment]] on a flight from Da Nang to [[Phu Bai Combat Base]] crashed into high ground killing all on board.<ref>{{cite news|title=33 on U.S. copter vanish in storm|newspaper=The New York Times|date=30 November 1971}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://aviation-safety.net/wikibase/76002|title=Accident Boeing CH-47C Chinook 68-15866|publisher=Aviation Safety Network|accessdate=16 November 2020}}</ref>

The U.S. Navy announced that the [[United States Navy SEALs]] had ended their combat role in the war and would be withdrawn in the next month. The SEALs had been kept in South Vietnam as a contingency force for Bright Light POW recovery operations.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Navy's 'Seals,' super-secret commandos, are quitting Vietnam|author=Craig Whitney|date=29 November 1971|page=2}}</ref>

;30 November
86 PAVN were killed in fighting northwest of Kontum and two U.S. observation helicopters were shot down in the fighting.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=2 U.S. helicopters down in Vietnam|date=30 November 1971|page=14}}</ref>

==December==
;1-2 December
The PAVN renewed its attacks north and northeast of Phnom Penh forcing ANK units to withdraw from Baray and Kompong Thmar.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Foe in Cambodia renews heavy attacks|date=2 December 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;2 December
ANK forces trying to reopen Route 6 were routed by the PAVN with approximately 10,000 ANK troops fleeing in disarray leaving {{convert|25|mi}} of Route 6 under PAVN control.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Cambodia's drive collapses in face of enemy attack|date=3 December 1971|page=1}}</ref>

Two U.S. helicopters collided while returning from an operation in the U Minh Forest killing four Americans.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Skirmishes in east Cambodia|date=3 December 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;3 December
PAVN forces attacked an RF/PF unit {{convert|48|mi}} northwest of Saigon killing 24 while losing 32 PAVN.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Heavy casualties reported|date=3 December 1971|page=3}}</ref>

The VC announced that they would observe three day ceasefires for Christmas and New Year and a four-day truce for Tết.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Vietcong announces truces for 3 holidays|date=3 December 1971|page=4}}</ref>

RVNAF UH-1 transports and helicopter gunships began flying support missions to the ANK forces under siege on Route 6.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=More clashes in east Cambodia reported|date=4 December 1971|page=2}}</ref>

;4 December
A [[General Accounting Office]] report largely attributed the growing refugee problem in Cambodia to U.S. bombing.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Refugee problem in Cambodia laid to Allied bombs|date=5 December 1971|page=1}}</ref>

The South Korean 2nd Marine Brigade departed South Vietnam.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Before Korean pullout|date=5 December 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;4-6 December
The PAVN attacked [[Bat Doeng]] {{convert|16|mi}} northwest of Phnom Penh killing approximately 50 ANK troops.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Heavy Cambodian casualties reported|date=6 December 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;5 December
The USAF turned over [[Phù Cát Air Base]] to the RVNAF.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=More MiG activity over Laos concerns U.S. Air Chief|date=6 December 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;6 December
ANK forces abandoned Bat Doeng and withdrew to a nearby base.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Cambodians flee village|date=7 December 1971|page=9}}</ref>

PAVN forces captured Saravane on the Boloven Plateau.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|385}}

The South Vietnamese government began a program to close girlie-bars that had catered to U.S. servicemen in central Saigon.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon Is Closing Sleazy Bars for G.I.'s|author=Gloria Emerson|date=7 December 1971|page=10}}</ref>

;7 December - May 1973
[[The Vinh wiretap]] was a CIA espionage operation to intercept North Vietnamese military telephone lines.<ref name=Conboy/>{{rp|381–6}}

;8 December
PAVN forces from the 52nd Regiment attacked the hamlets of Srei Ngei and Kleah Sanday on Route south of Phnom Penh. The PAVN also attacked Phnombaset {{convert|8|mi}} northwest of Phnom Penh and the Kambal training center {{convert|10|mi}} west of the city.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Bitter fighting reported as foe opens new drive on Pnompenh|date=9 December 1971|page=4}}</ref>

The main body of the 4th Battalion, Royal Australian Regiment departed South Vietnam, ending Australia's combat participation in the war.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Thieu Bids Farewell To Australian Troops|date=9 December 1971|page=4}}</ref>

;9 December
The 138th session of the Paris Peace Talks ended without the parties agreeing the next meeting date.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|385}}

;11 December
A USAF F-105G escorting a B-52 mission over Laos was shot down by a SAM near the Mụ Giạ Pass.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. fighter downed over Laos by a North Vietnamese missile|date=11 December 1971|page=6}}</ref>

ARVN units killed 127 PAVN for the loss of 22 ARVN in fighting near Dam Be, Cambodia in the largest battle of its latest Cambodian operation. 200 civilians were reported killed in Suong in a misguided airstrike.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon reports heaviest battle of its operation in Cambodia|date=11 December 1971|page=7}}</ref>

ANK forces abandoned Phnombaset after the hill position was encircled by PAVN forces.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Cambodians retreat from hill near Pnompenh's defense line|date=12 December 1971|page=21}}</ref>

;13-14 December
ARVN Airborne and Rangers supported by armor began an offensive to capture the Chup rubber plantation, a base area for the PAVN 7th and 9th Divisions. ARVN and ANK forces captured Chup on the 14th after PAVN forces abandoned the area.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|385}}

;14 December
ANK forces fought with PAVN at Taing Kauk, {{convert|48|mi}} north of Phnom Penh.<ref name=NYT15Dec>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Chup, in Cambodia, seized, Saigon says|date=15 December 1971|page=3}}</ref>

A U.S. Army U-21 #67-18041 with six Americans onboard disappeared on a flight between Phu Bai and Da Nang, the wreckage was later found in [[Da Nang Bay]].<ref name=NYT15Dec/><ref name=U-21/>

;16 December
A USAF F-4 was hit by antiaircraft fire while bombing Phnom Baset and attempted to make an emergency landing at Pochentong Airport, but the crew was forced to eject on approach.<ref name=NYT17Dec>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. F-4 downed at Pnompenh|date=17 December 1971|page=3}}</ref>

PAVN forces attacked the ANK at Taing Kauk and Prakham north-northeast of Phnom Penh.<ref name=NYT17Dec/>

Secretary of the Air Force [[Robert Seamans]] said that reconnaissance showed a buildup of trucks in Hanoi and Haiphong indicated a coming PAVN offensive. He also provided statistics to counter the charge that the air war in Indochina was expanding.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Seamans says build-up at Hanoi suggests push|date=17 December 1971|page=3}}</ref>

;17 December
Oran Henderson was acquitted on all charges relating to his role in the Mỹ Lai massacre.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Col. Henderson acquitted in last of the Mylai cases|author=Douglas Robinson|date=18 December 1971|page=1}}</ref>

The ARVN withdrew from Chup and Suong.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|386}}

PAVN forces attacked Vihta Suor {{convert|10|mi}} north of Phnom Penh while continuing their attacks on Taing Kauk and Prakham, blowing up a bridge between the two towns. The Cambodian government issued a new security decree prohibiting political gatherings and protests. Senior U.S., South Vietnamese and Cambodian officials met in Phnom Penh to discuss the deteriorating security situation around the capital.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Allied chiefs meet in Pnompenh as Cambodian posts weaken|date=18 December 1971|page=9}}</ref>

A USAF F-4 was shot down by a SAM near the Mụ Giạ Pass.<ref name=Hartsook/>{{rp|31–2}}

;17 December - 30 January 1972
[[Campaign Z]] was a PAVN combined arms operation against the RLA base at Long Tieng. The PAVN used [[T-34]] tanks and [[130 mm towed field gun M1954 (M-46)|130mm field guns]] for the first time supported by VPAF fighter jets. The PAVN were able to temporarily seize high ground and shell Long Tieng before being pushed back.<ref name=Conboy/>{{rp|323–34}}

;18 December
''The New York Times'' reported that the U.S. Army in Vietnam was discharging between 1,000 and 2,000 soldiers for heroin usage despite the Nixon Administration pledging that heroin users would be retained in the Army where they would be treated.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Army discharges many addicts in Vietnam despite U.S. pledges|date=19 December 1971|page=2}}</ref>

A VPAF MiG-21 downed the first U.S. aircraft since June 1968, a USAF F-4D flying a combat air patrol approximately {{convert|70|miles}} west/northwest of [[Tho Xuan Airport|Bái Thượng Air Base]]. Two USAF F-4s searching for the crew of the downed aircraft were engaged by two MiG-21s, ultimately resulting in the ejection of the two F-4 crews for lack of fuel and loss of their aircraft.<ref name=Hartsook/>{{rp|31–2}} Another F-4 was shot down by anti-aircraft fire near Long Tieng. Four U.S. crewmen were captured.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Hanoi challenges air power of U.S.|author=Craig Whitney|date=20 December 1971|page=1}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. confirms the loss of four jets over Laos|author=Craig Whitney|date=21 December 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;20 December
The South Vietnamese government announced 24 hour ceasefires for Christmas, New Year and Tết and said that the longer ceasefires announced by the VC were intended to allow for their recuperation and resupply.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. and Saigon to mark 3 daylong holiday truces|date=21 December 1971|page=4}}</ref>

;21 December
An ANK task force reached Prakham relieving the garrison there.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|386}}

''The New York Times'' reported on the declining morale within the ANK in the face of the hardened PAVN opposition.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Confidence on War Ebbs In the Cambodian Army|author=Gloria Emerson|date=23 December 1971|page=4}}</ref>

;22 December
U.S. jets attacked a North Vietnamese antiaircraft radar site in the 101st "protective reaction" strike of the war.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. jets attack radar site within 82 miles of Hanoi|date=23 December 1971|page=1}}</ref>

''The New York Times'' reported that information about the war was becoming increasingly difficult to obtain, particularly since MACV had stopped briefings on South Vietnamese operations earlier in the year.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=News policy for the Vietnam War: As little as possible|author=Craig Whitney|date=23 December 1971|page=5}}</ref>

The Soviet Union accused China of backing U.S. policy in Vietnam.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|386}}

;23 December
Secretary of State Rogers said that the U.S. would continue to conduct airstrikes in Laos and Cambodia into 1972 in order to disrupt North Vietnamese offensives.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Rogers declares U.S. will continue Asian air strikes|author=Terence Smith|date=24 December 1971|page=1}}</ref>

[[Bob Hope]] met with a North Vietnamese diplomat in [[Vientiane]] and offered US$10 million to a North Vietnamese children's charity to secure the release of U.S. POWs.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|386}}

;25 December
South Vietnam reported 26 PAVN/VC ceasefire breaches with 13 ARVN and 11 PAVN/VC killed. U.S. jets conducted their 106th "protective reaction" strike of the year against a SAM radar site {{convert|5|mi}} from the Laos border.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Saigon reports violations of the holiday cease-fire|date=25 December 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;26-8 December
Sixteen members of Vietnam Veterans Against the War occupied the [[Statue of Liberty]] before eventually leaving after a court order.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|387}}

;26-30 December
In response to the recent attacks on U.S. aircraft, Nixon ordered [[Operation Proud Deep Alpha]], an intensive five-day bombing campaign against airfields, antiaircraft sites and other military targets in North Vietnam just north of the DMZ above the [[17th parallel north]].<ref name=Asselin/>{{rp|29}}<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|387}}

;27 December
''The New York Times'' reported on growing campaigns within the U.S. for an amnesty for deserters and draft evaders.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=National Drive Seeks Amnesty For Deserters and Draft Evaders|author=Bill Kovach|date=28 December 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;28 December
ARVN units killed 28 PAVN in a skirmish in the Central Highlands.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=U.S. copter shot down|date=30 December 1971|page=3}}</ref>

80 members of Vietnam Veterans Against the War were arrested on the steps of the [[Lincoln Memorial]].<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|387}}

;28-9 December
PAVN and Pathet Lao forces captured Paksong securing control of the Boloven Plateau but they abandon it to the RLA on the 29th.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|386}}

;30 December
Daniel Ellsberg was again indicted on Federal criminal charges relating to the release of the Pentagon Papers.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|title=Ellsberg indicted again in Pentagon case|author=Steven Roberts|date=31 December 1971|page=1}}</ref>

;30 December - 16 March 1972
[[Operation Maharat]] was the RLA defense of the Route 7 and 13 intersection at Sala Phoun Khoun. After being initially pushed out the RLA counterattacked and seized the area.<ref name=Conboy/>{{rp|331}}

; 31 December
The number of U.S. military personnel in South Vietnam totaled 156,800.<ref name=Willbanks/>{{rp|387}}

